und GKR kernel.\n\nThe main ideas are batching multiple interaction sets together, caching redundant trace reads in registers, or overlapping the GKR evaluation with other work. But I need to reconcile the GPU timing numbers—439ms of total GPU time across 486 instances with 8 concurrent streams suggests there's overhead beyond just kernel execution that's pushing the wall-clock time to 522ms.\n\nLooking at the LogUp GKR breakdown, the input evaluation accounts for about 500ms of GPU time, while the fractional sumcheck rounds (compute_round_and_revert, bit reversal, multifold, and precompute operations) add another 212ms sequentially. That's already 712ms of GPU work, which exceeds the 522ms wall time, so these must be overlapping across streams. I'm confusing the timing breakdown here. The LogUp GKR wall time of 522ms must include both the GKR input evaluation across 8 concurrent streams (which should be around 55ms but likely skewed by load imbalance) and the sequential fractional sumcheck layers, with the input evaluation historically taking 300-320ms based on previous profiling data.\n\nLet me step back and think about how multi-stream execution actually works on the GPU - with 128 SMs on the RTX 4090, multiple small kernels can run concurrently on different SMs rather than purely time-sharing, so the wall time depends on actual GPU capacity utilization rather than just dividing total GPU time by stream count. Instead of trying to reverse-engineer wall time from these GPU metrics, I should rely on what the previous reports show about actual execution time.\n\nLooking at the most recent overlap-logup report, LogUp GKR takes 526ms at APC 300, with the GKR input evaluation down to around 318ms and fractional sumcheck rounds accounting for the remaining 200ms. The trace commit phase is a major bottleneck at 247ms - the Poseidon2 hashing kernel alone consumes 231ms across 25 instances, and then the Merkle tree construction follows. I'm examining where previous optimiz