loor—even if I could scale the other components infinitely, STARK excluding trace can't go below that. So why aren't Round 0, MLE Rounds, and Stacked Reduction scaling with APC count? Round 0 evaluates per-AIR constraints, and with APCs there are 623 AIRs instead of 99. Each AIR is smaller, so the total work scales, but the per-AIR overhead from kernel launches, setup, and memory transfers stays constant. That overhead alone is probably around 125ms across all AIRs, leaving the actual kernel work much smaller than expected.\n\nMLE Rounds actually get worse with APCs because there are more traces to fold, and even though each trace is smaller, the total work per round increases with AIR count. Stacked Reduction is already heavily optimized and bottlenecked by CPU-side NTT reconstruction time rather than GPU work.\n\nThe main improvement opportunities are reducing Round 0's per-AIR overhead, optimizing MLE's trace folding, and potentially parallelizing more of Stacked Reduction's sequential loops. The MLE rounds are inherently sequential with each depending on the previous one, and at APC 300 there are roughly 20 rounds (one per sumcheck variable) taking about 8.7ms each. The main operations—fold, interpolation, and monomial evaluation—are already batched into descriptor-array launches, so further speedup would require kernel fusion to combine multiple operations per round. Moving on to trace commitment, which is dominated by Poseidon2 hashing and takes 247ms. 231ms. This is a large kernel (25 instances, some up to 45ms). Could we:\n   - Multi-stream the Poseidon2 hashing across segments? (likely already single-stream)\n   - Overlap Poseidon2 hashing with something else?\n   - Optimize the Poseidon2 kernel itself? (algorithmic/memory optimization)\n   \n   At APC 0, Trace Commit is 532ms (also a significant fraction). The poseidon2 kernel at APC 0 would be fewer instances but each larger (fewer, bigger AIRs).\n\n   **D. Optimize LogUp GKR further (522ms)**: The GKR in