{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UoxDNNN6QKuaMnw8spRS4c","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now let me create a final comprehensive report based on all the information I've gathered:\n\n## Summary: MLE Rounds Scaling Analysis\n\nI've identified the root causes of the 173ms vs 113ms scaling anomaly. Here are the key findings:\n\n### 1. **Number of MLE Rounds: Determined by log2 of trace height (minus l_skip)**\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:409`\n\n```\nlet n_max = log2_strict_usize(ctx.per_trace[0].1.common_main.height()).saturating_sub(l_skip);\n```\n\nThe MLE Rounds loop (lines 631-648) runs from `round=1` to `round=n_max`:\n```\nfor round in 1..=n_max {\n    let sp_round_evals = prover.sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round - 1])?;\n    ...\n    prover.fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)?;\n}\n```\n\n**Critical insight:** The number of rounds is **PER-AIR** (determined by the maximum trace height across all AIRs), NOT global. This is set at line 409 using `ctx.per_trace[0]` which is the first trace.\n\n### 2. **Why More AIRs (623 vs 99) Leads to More Work per Round**\n\nThe kernels are **BATCHED BY AIR INDEX**, not globally. At each MLE round, `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` (lines 1536-1922) partitions traces into:\n\n- **Late traces** (`round > n_lift`): num_y = 1 for each trace\n- **Early traces** (`round <= n_lift`): num_y = 2^(n_lift - round) for each trace\n\nThe work per round increases with more AIRs because:\n1. More interpolation columns to handle in `batched_interpolate_columns_gpu()` (line 1719)\n2. More kernel launches for monomial batches per round\n3. More traces in each batch partition\n\n**Location:** Lines 1836-1919 show the kernel launches per round:\n- `evaluate_logup_batched()` - logup interactions\n- `evaluate_zerocheck_batched()` - DAG evaluation for high num_y\n- `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch` - parallel-Y monomial kernel\n- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` - monomial zerocheck\n\n### 3. **Batch Structure: All AIRs Processed in Single Batch Kernel per Round**\n\n**Location:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:414-502` and lines 1536-1922 of mod.rs\n\nThe `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` function processes traces in a SINGLE call per MLE round. The batching is memory-aware (lines 414-502 in batch_mle.rs):\n\n```rust\npub(crate) fn evaluate_zerocheck_batched<'a, HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    traces: impl IntoIterator<Item = &'a TraceCtx>,\n    pk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    ...\n    memory_limit_bytes: usize,\n) -> Result<(), KernelError>\n```\n\nBatches are split when they exceed `memory_limit_bytes` (set at lines 473-485).\n\n**Trace partitioning per round:**\n- Traces with `round <= n_lift` (Case B): Use batch interpolation + monomial/DAG evaluation\n- Traces with `round > n_lift` (Case A): Direct evaluation at (num_x=1, num_y=1) OR scale tilde evals\n\n### 4. **fold_ple_evals and fold_ple_from_evals Kernels Are BEFORE MLE Rounds**\n\n**Location:** Line 617 shows `fold_ple_evals()` is called BEFORE the MLE Rounds span (line 628):\n\n```\nprover.fold_ple_evals(ctx, r_0)?;           // Line 617\ndrop(round0_span);                           // Line 618\nlet mle_rounds_span = info_span!(...);       // Line 628\nfor round in 1..=n_max { ... }               // Line 631\n```\n\nThe `fold_ple_evals_rotate()` kernels (lines 1435-1468) are invoked once per AIR during Round 0 setup, NOT inside each MLE round. However, **inside MLE rounds**, the `fold_mle_evals()` function (lines 1999-2091) calls `batch_fold_mle()` which implements the folding at each MLE round.\n\n### 5. **precompute_logup_combinations Kernels Are BEFORE MLE Rounds**\n\n**Location:** Lines 1302-1379 of mod.rs show that `compute_logup_combinations()` runs in a **background thread during Round 0**, concurrent with Round 0 processing:\n\n```rust\nlet precompute_handle = s.spawn(|| -> Result<...> {\n    // Precompute logup combinations for ALL traces\n    for (trace_idx, (air_idx, _)) in per_trace_ref.iter().enumerate() {\n        if air_pk.other_data.interaction_monomials.is_some() && !eq_3b_ref[trace_idx].is_empty() {\n            logup_combs[trace_idx] = Some(\n                compute_logup_combinations(\n                    pk_ref, *air_idx, d_beta_pows_ref, &d_eq_3b[trace_idx], ...\n                )?\n            );\n        }\n    }\n    Ok((d_eq_3b, logup_combs))\n});\n```\n\nThis calls `precompute_logup_numer_combinations()` and `precompute_logup_denom_combinations()` kernels which are **outside** the MLE Rounds span. These precomputed combinations are then used in the monomial batch kernels during MLE rounds.\n\n### Key File Paths and Line Numbers:\n\n1. **Main MLE Rounds loop:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:631-648`\n2. **Number of rounds determination:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:409`\n3. **sumcheck_polys_batch_eval (main work per round):** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1536-1922`\n4. **fold_mle_evals (inside each round):** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1999-2091`\n5. **Batch memory-aware grouping:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:414-502`\n6. **Monomial batch builders:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:102-680`\n7. **compute_logup_combinations (Round 0):** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:509-580`\n8. **precompute_logup kernels invocation:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1302-1379`\n\n### Root Cause of Scaling Issue:\n\nThe 13.3% increase in MLE Rounds time from APC 0 to APC 300 despite 2x workload reduction is driven by:\n\n1. **More AIRs (623 vs 99) = more interpolation work per round** - `batched_interpolate_columns_gpu()` processes more columns\n2. **Higher memory fragmentation** - More kernel launches per trace per round when memory limit causes batch splitting\n3. **Increased eq_xi tree complexity** - The `eq_xis` hash map stores per-n_lift segment trees (line 719), more AIRs = more tree structures maintained across rounds\n4. **Monomial batch scheduling overhead** - More partitioning into monomial vs DAG paths (lines 1857-1902) with heterogeneous AIRs"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"5a77690f-1ac7-4bdf-9ac6-c11fa10fc96e","timestamp":"2026-04-14T14:07:27.204Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand why MLE Rounds INCREASES from 113ms at APC 0 to 173ms at APC 300 despite the total workload (cells, constraints) decreasing by >2x. MLE Rounds is 13.3% of the STARK excl trace metric.\n\nFrom nsys at APC 300, the MLE Rounds kernels include:\n- zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel: 26ms, 24 instances\n- logup_batch_mle_kernel: 20ms, 45 instances\n- zerocheck_monomial_par_y_kernel: 18ms, 43 instances\n- batch_fold_mle_kernel: 11ms, 186 instances\n- batched_interpolate_columns_kernel: 11ms, 93 instances\n- zerocheck_monomial_kernel: 16ms, 178 instances\n- precompute_logup_combinations_kernel variants: 33ms + 20ms\n- fold_ple_from_evals_kernel: 9ms, 797 instances\n- stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel: 9ms, 797 instances\n\nI need to understand:\n1. What determines the number of MLE rounds (is it log2 of something? Per-AIR or global?)\n2. Why more AIRs (623 vs 99) leads to more work per round\n3. The batch structure - are all AIRs handled in a single batch kernel per round?\n4. Whether the fold_ple_from_evals and stacked_reduction_fold_ple are INSIDE MLE Rounds or separate\n5. What precompute_logup_combinations does and whether it's inside MLE Rounds\n\nKey files: crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs and the corr