n decouple when the commitment finishes from when Round 0 starts computing, there might be room to overlap them on separate GPU streams.\n\nThe real bottleneck though is the fractional sumcheck overhead. There's a significant gap between the actual GPU kernel time (~191ms) and total wall time (~460ms), suggesting the ~270ms difference comes from per-round costs like buffer construction, D2H syncs, and CUDA API overhead. With hundreds of sumcheck rounds across both segments, even small per-round overheads add up quickly.\n\nI'm digging into the D2H sync mechanism now — specifically how `reconstruct_s_evals` calls `to_host()`, which does an async memcpy followed by a stream sync that waits for all preceding work to complete. This sync might be blocking on more than just the 32-byte transfer itself.\n\nThe real bottleneck could be the kernels launching before each sync. In the early GKR rounds with massive element counts, a single kernel processing 2^28 elements could take several milliseconds. I need to reconsider which rounds are actually the most expensive — the tree structure means the largest data is at the top, not the bottom. confused. The tree has 2^n leaves. The GKR processes from the root down. Round 1 processes the root level (small). Round total_rounds-1 processes near the leaves (large).\n\nActually, let me re-read the code. Line 668: `let mut pq_size = 2 << round;`\n\nSo at round 1: pq_size = 4\nAt round 2: pq_size = 8\nAt round j: pq_size = 2^(j+1)\nAt round total_rounds-1 (last): pq_size = 2^total_rounds = total_leaves\n\nSo the LAST rounds process the most data. This means the kernel time per round increases with j. The early rounds (1-5) are very fast, and the late rounds (25-28) process hundreds of millions of elements.\n\nFor the precompute-M strategy, the buffer shrinks as we process windows within each round. The first window of each GKR round handles the largest pq_size, then subsequent windows work on progressively smaller data. The expensive la