hough individual AIRs might be smaller at APC 300, if the largest trace maintains a similar height, the total number of rounds stays roughly constant—which would explain why MLE time increases despite fewer total cells. I'm seeing that the xi vector gets extended to length l_skip + n_global after the fractional sumcheck, but the MLE rounds themselves only use n_max, which could be smaller than n_global. Let me trace through the LogupZerocheckGpu code more carefully to understand exactly how many MLE rounds actually happen and what n_max controls.\n\nI've been digging into this for a while, so let me shift gears and start thinking about optimization strategies. The main bottleneck I'm seeing is the per-GKR-round eq_buffer construction overhead—I could potentially reduce this by building it incrementally rather than from scratch each round.\n\nThe eq_buffer (SqrtEqLayers) gets rebuilt completely at each GKR round, which means O(n²/2) total kernel launches and allocations across all rounds. If I maintain it across rounds and just add new layers incrementally, I could cut that down to O(n) launches. That's probably worth 8-15ms per segment, though the actual overhead might only be around 8ms total, which is modest.\n\nAnother angle is the Trace Commit phase—I could split the stacked matrix into vertical strips and build partial Merkle trees on separate CUDA streams, then combine them at the top. This would let the GPU work on multiple Poseidon2 hashes concurrently.\n\nFor Idea 3, instead of rebuilding the eq_buffer each round, I could pre-compute all the layers upfront at the start of fractional sumcheck and just index into them as needed. Since xi_prev grows by one element per round, each round's layers are just a prefix of the final set. But I need to think through how drop_layer() fits into this during each GKR round.\n\nThe key insight is that eq_buffer only holds pointers to device buffers, so drop_layer() just removes entries from a Vec. If I pre-compute everything, I can keep all layers alive and use an index instead. Let me trace through the actual cost: for ~29 rounds, at round j I'm building SqrtEqLayers from xi_prev[1..j], which calls EqEvalLayers::new twice (once for low_n, once for high_n). Each of those does a host-to-device transfer of the base values, so I'm looking at eliminating those repeated transfers and allocations across all rounds.\n\nBreaking down the per-round cost: each EqEvalLayers::new does one H2D transfer (~1μs), then j-1 kernel launches (~5-10μs each) and j-1 device buffer allocations (~0.5μs each). That's roughly (j-1) × 15μs per round. Summing across all 28 rounds gives me about 378 × 0.015ms ≈ 5.7ms total. That's not significant enough to justify a complex pre-computation scheme.\n\nLet me shift focus to something more impactful — the D2H synchronization in the fractional sumcheck might be a better target for async optimization with double buffering.\n\nThe current flow has each inner sumcheck round launch a kernel, run a reduction, then block on to_host() while the GPU sits idle during CPU reconstruction and transcript work. I could try making that D2H copy asynchronous using CUDA events instead of a full stream sync, letting the next round's setup happen in parallel. But there's a hard dependency: the next round's fold kernel needs the challenge r_t from the transcript, which only comes after the current round's CPU work finishes. So true pipelining isn't possible unless I could somehow launch the next kernel with a placeholder for r_t and...\n\nActually, the sumcheck protocol itself prevents that kind of speculation. But there's another angle: instead of launching 41 separate kernels per GKR round, I could fuse the M-build, the three evals, and the multifold into a single kernel, cutting that down to just 9 kernels total per round.\n\nThe catch is that the fused kernel would need device-to-host transfers between eval rounds to update the transcript, which becomes the real bottleneck.\n\nWait—the precompute-M approach already handles this elegantly. Rather than running w separate fold-and-compute cycles (each requiring a D2H sync), it precomputes the M matrix once, then each eval round only touches that small O(4^w)=64 element matrix instead of the full buffer. So the optimization is already baked in.\n\nLet me step back and consider something completely different. What if I could pipeline the fractional sumcheck with the MLE rounds themselves? The fractional sumcheck produces claims that feed into MLE, so theoretically I could start MLE round 0 while the fractional sumcheck is still working through later GKR rounds.\n\nThe dependency is real though—the fractional sumcheck generates xi (random challenges) incrementally, one element per GKR round, and the MLE rounds need those xi values to compute weight evaluations. But the MLE loop only needs xi[l_skip..l_skip+n_global], so maybe I can start the early MLE rounds using the first few xi values before the full sumcheck finishes.\n\nLooking at how each MLE round samples...\n\nActually, wait. Each MLE round needs a new random challenge r_round derived from both zerocheck and logup evaluations, and the logup weights depend on the full xi vector. So all xi values have to be ready before the first MLE round even starts—I can't pipeline them.\n\nNow I'm thinking about moving the Fiat-Shamir transcript processing to the GPU side. The per-round CPU work in the fractional sumcheck is just copying a couple of field elements from device to host, reconstructing one polynomial evaluation, and observing it.\n\nIf I could do all that on the GPU instead—have a kernel reconstruct the evaluation, update the transcript, and sample the next challenge—then r would stay on GPU for the next round without any host-device transfers. The Poseidon2 hash function is already implemented on GPU for the Merkle tree, so I could reuse that for the transcript hashing. This would eliminate the per-round synchronization bottleneck entirely.\n\nThe real question is whether the CPU work is actually the limiting factor. The transcript processing itself is just a few microseconds per round, so even if I eliminate all the D2H transfers, I'm only saving maybe 200ms if each sync costs around 0.5ms in GPU idle time. But I should verify whether that's actually where the 270ms overhead is coming from.\n\nLooking at the actual GPU kernel timings, the fractional sumcheck is spending significant time in compute_round_and_revert (70ms), compute_round_and_fold_inplace (9ms), multifold (47ms), precompute_m_build (45ms), and bit_rev_frac_build_k2 (50ms). I need to understand which of these kernels are actually being blocked by the CPU transcript processing versus running independently. Looking at the fractional sumcheck wall time versus GPU kernel time, there's a massive 309ms overhead that needs accounting for. The main culprits appear to be the per-round operations like buffer construction and synchronization, plus memory management overhead across the 56 total rounds, though I'm realizing the tree building might already be factored into the GPU time estimates. bit_rev 50ms + two_layers 12ms = 62ms) is PER fractional_sumcheck_gpu call. With 2 calls (2 segments), that's 124ms of tree build GPU time. This IS sequential (single stream). So the tree build alone is 124ms of wall time (≈ GPU time for sequential operations on one stream).\n\nAnd the actual sumcheck rounds GPU time is 233 - 62 = ~171ms across all calls (app + recursion). For app proof, maybe ~120ms.\n\nSo fractional sumcheck wall time breakdown (459ms, app proof):\n- Tree build: ~62ms × 2 segments ≈ 124ms\n- Sumcheck rounds GPU time: ~120ms\n- Overhead: ~215ms\n\nThat's roughly 0.54ms of overhead per inner sumcheck round across ~400 rounds. I need to trace where this is coming from. Each round involves a small kernel launch for precompute_m_eval, a D2H transfer of the sum evals, CPU-side reconstruction and transcript operations, and eq table manipulations. The kernel itself is tiny, but there's likely significant overhead from the CUDA driver API calls and stream synchronization happening per round. Looking at the eq_buffer construction more carefully, I see that for each GKR round, SqrtEqLayers builds from the previous xi values and instantiates EqEvalLayers twice—once for the low variables and once for the high. Each EqEvalLayers allocation involves creating n+1 device buffers and launching n kernels, starting with a single-element buffer for the base case and then allocating exponentially larger buffers for each subsequent layer. locAsync\n  - Launch eq_hypercube_interleaved_stage_ext kernel → processes 2^i elements\n\nWait, actually each layer is its OWN DeviceBuffer. So for n layers:\n- n+1 DeviceBuffer allocations (sizes: 1, 2, 4, ..., 2^n)\n- n kernel launches (sizes: 1, 2, 4, ..., 2^(n-1))\n- 1 H2D copy of size 1\n\nAnd SqrtEqLayers::from_xi with j-1 total elements creates:\n- low: low_n = (j-1)/2 layers → low_n+1 allocs, low_n kernels\n- high: high_n = (j-1) - low_n layers → high_n+1 allocs, high_n kernels\n\nTotal per GKR round: (j-1) kernel launches and (j+1) allocations. At the end of each round, the eq_buffer gets dropped, freeing all (j+1) DeviceBuffers with cudaFreeAsync calls. Across all 29 GKR rounds, this totals 378 kernel launches, 434 allocations, and 434 frees—roughly 1246 CUDA API calls just for managing the eq_buffer per fractional_sumcheck_gpu call.\n\nWith two calls, that's 2492 API calls. Estimating ~1μs per malloc/free and ~10μs per kernel launch gives about 9.4ms total, which still doesn't account for the 180ms gap. The missing time likely comes from launch latency on small kernels, the full stream synchronization required by each D2H copy, and fragmentation from many small operations scattered across the CUDA stream.\n\nLooking at the precompute-M approach more carefully, the eval_round kernels operate on tiny datasets—just 64 EF elements for the M matrix. These small kernels have launch overhead that dominates their execution time, and with roughly 500-700 of them across all rounds, that's another 5-7ms. The H2D copies of eq evaluation data are also a culprit—there are hundreds of these per fractional sumcheck, each involving a small transfer plus potential synchronization overhead that compounds across the entire computation.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the sheer volume of CUDA API calls. For two fractional_sumcheck_gpu calls at APC 300, I'm looking at thousands of operations: hundreds of eq_hypercube kernels, sumcheck and reduction kernels, D2H copies with syncs, H2D copies for tables and buffers, and alloc/free operations. Even though each individual call is fast—1-10 microseconds—the cumulative overhead across 5000+ API calls adds up to 25-100ms depending on average latency per call.\n\nThe key is to eliminate these calls entirely rather than just optimize them. Pre-computing all eq_buffer layers once and reusing them would cut out roughly 756 kernel launches and 868 allocs/frees per fractional_sumcheck_gpu call, saving 20-40ms per call. That's a meaningful improvement worth pursuing.\n\nThe challenge is that xi_prev grows dynamically during GKR rounds as new mu values are sampled from the transcript, so I can't pre-compute everything upfront. But I can build the layers incrementally—at each round j, I already have all layers from the previous round and just need to add one new layer for the new mu value. The tricky part is handling how the sqrt decomposition splits these layers across rounds. into `low` and `high` halves, and the split point changes as the total size grows. But we could avoid the sqrt decomposition and use a simpler structure.\n\nActually, looking at the code more carefully, the eq_buffer is used in the sumcheck rounds (passed to compute kernels). The sumcheck kernels need eq(xi, x) evaluations. The SqrtEqLayers provides these via sqrt decomposition.\n\nWait, there's another option: instead of rebuilding SqrtEqLayers, we could build it incrementally by keeping all previously-computed layers alive and only adding new ones.\n\nLet me trace through how the split sizes evolve as we add elements. At round j, we have j-1 elements in xi_prev, and at round j+1 we have j elements. The low/high split changes each time, but I can work out the pattern by checking specific cases: with 1 element we get low=[], high=[mu_2]; with 2 elements it's low=[mu_3], high=[mu_2]; with 3 elements it's low=[mu_3], high=[mu_2, mu_4]. The key insight is whether we can reuse the previous layers or if the split forces us to rebuild. The low half changes every round since it's either adding new elements or shifting, so it can't be directly reused. However, when we add a new element to the array, we're introducing a new layer that depends on all previous layers, and I can compute this new layer from the existing layer and the previous computation.\n\nWhen the high half grows by one, I can reuse all existing layers and just compute the additional one. For the low half, since the values shift with the split point, I'd need to rebuild it from scratch each round—but since it's smaller, that's manageable.\n\nLooking at the actual values: at j=5, the low half uses elements from positions 2-4 reversed, but at j=6 it uses positions 3-5 reversed. These are completely different inputs, so the low layers can't be reused directly and must be recomputed.\n\nFor the high half though, there's a pattern: when high_n grows from j=5 to j=6, the new reversed sequence [mu_4, mu_3, mu_2] has the old sequence [mu_3, mu_2] as a suffix, meaning I can keep the existing layers and just prepend a new one for mu_4. When high_n stays the same, the layers are fully reusable. So the optimization is to conditionally reuse the high layers when possible while always rebuilding the low layers.\n\nThis cuts the kernel launches roughly in half—from ~378 down to ~189—saving about 1.9ms per call from reduced kernel overhead, plus another 0.4ms from fewer allocations.\n\nBut that's still only around 2.3ms per call, or 4.6ms total across both fractional_sumcheck_gpu invocations. Not enough to explain the 270ms gap.\n\nLet me step back and consider whether the overhead is actually coming from somewhere else entirely. Maybe the tree build and inherent GPU kernel execution time are the real bottlenecks, not wasteful redundant work. Let me recalculate what's actually happening across the two fractional_sumcheck_gpu calls.\n\nThe tree build phase is taking significant time—bit_rev_frac_build_k2 shows up multiple times across different recursion stages. With 2 app proof segments plus leaf and inner recursion calls, I'm seeing around 6 instances total, which accounts for roughly 50ms. That's a substantial chunk of the 270ms right there.\n\nThen there's the sumcheck rounds themselves. The compute_round_and_revert kernel is taking about 40ms for the app proof portion, multifold is around 30ms, and precompute_m_build is another 30ms. These aren't trivial—they're the actual computational work the GPU needs to do.\n\nAdding in the eq_buffer construction at 6ms, CUDA API overhead around 20ms, and buffer management at roughly 8ms, I'm getting a total estimate of about 214ms for the fractional sumcheck. But the actual wall time is 452ms—that's a gap of 238ms that I need to account for. Something's consuming significantly more time than my bottom-up estimates suggest.\n\nLet me reconsider whether I'm underestimating the GPU kernel time for the app proof phase. Looking at the nsys data, I need to figure out what fraction of the total kernel time is actually spent on app proof versus the other recursion stages. At APC 300, the app proof dominates with 2 segments processing around 623 AIRs with complex constraints, while the leaf and inner recursion stages are much smaller. The fractional sumcheck for app proof involves a tree build for 2^29 leaves across 2 segments with roughly 29 rounds each—that's substantial work that might be taking longer than my kernel time estimates account for.\n\nThe bit_rev_frac_build_k2 kernel is particularly expensive since it's handling 537M FracExt elements (about 17 GB of data) with random-access bit-reversal and tree layer construction involving multiplications. Even at high bandwidth, this should take 30-50ms per segment just for that kernel alone. But the nsys profile shows only 50ms total across all 6 instances of bit_rev_frac_build_k2, which suggests most of those instances are indeed from the app proof phase—the math roughly checks out if 2 of them are taking 30ms each.\n\nLooking at the kernel timing distribution more carefully, the 6 instances average about 8ms each, but they're probably not uniform—the largest app proof segments likely take around 20ms while the smaller recursion instances are just 1-2ms. Stepping back, the top kernels sum to roughly 3100ms of GPU time, but the total benchmark runs for about 5700ms with only 55% GPU utilization, so there's significant idle time or memory bandwidth bottlenecks happening.\n\nFor the fractional sumcheck at APC 300, I need to account for the fact that app proof segments dominate the kernel time despite being fewer in count. The 141 instances of compute_round_and_revert break down to about 56 from app proof (28 GKR rounds × 2) and 85 from recursion, but since app proof operates on much larger data (pq_size up to 2^29), those 56 instances probably consume 90% of the 70ms total. I can estimate the app proof fractional sumcheck GPU time by attributing most of the kernel time to the large-data instances: bit_rev_frac_build_k2 contributes roughly 40ms of its 50ms total, and frac_build_tree_two_layers adds another 8ms.\n\nTallying up all the app proof fractional sumcheck kernels—bit_rev_frac_build_k2, frac_build_tree_two_layers, compute_round_and_revert, compute_round_and_fold_inplace, multifold, and the precompute_m kernels—I get around 211ms of pure GPU kernel execution time. Adding overhead from CUDA API calls, eq_buffer operations, device-to-host syncs, and miscellaneous costs brings the total to roughly 256ms, yet the measured wall time is 452ms, leaving a significant gap I need to account for.\n\nLooking more closely at the profile, I'm wondering if there's something expensive happening with the DeviceBuffer allocations. Specifically, the work_buffer and m_buffer get allocated fresh in each GKR round, and those cudaMallocAsync calls might be adding hidden latency that isn't showing up as kernel time.\n\nActually, wait—I'm seeing that work_buffer and tmp_block_sums are allocated once before the loop and reused, which is good. But eq_buffer is being recreated every round from the xi_prev data, which accounts for maybe 9ms total across all rounds. That's not the culprit either.\n\nNow I'm looking at the copy_from_device calls at the end of each GKR round—those are pulling data back from the GPU, and that might be where the real overhead is hiding. I'm going to run the fractional sumcheck benchmark directly to get accurate timing rather than trying to reverse-engineer it from analysis. Looking at my top optimization candidates, pre-computing eq_buffer layers incrementally could save 5-10ms with low complexity, but that's probably too small to matter. Overlapping the tree build with input evaluation won't work since the tree needs the full input first. GPU-side transcript handling for fractional sumcheck has the most potential at 50-200ms impact, but the complexity is prohibitive for prototyping right now.\n\nMulti-stream hashing for Trace Commit's Merkle tree could save 30-80ms, though the hash kernels might already be saturating the GPU. Pre-staging all the static per-AIR data and per-segment data at keygen time looks more promising—I'd expect 20-40ms savings since we're batching the full data package rather than just rules like before. Fusing operations within MLE is another angle worth exploring.\n\nFor the MLE rounds, I could combine fold, interpolation, and evaluation into a single kernel to cut down on launch overhead, though the complexity is high for what might only be 15-30ms of gains. On the D2H copy side, I'm thinking about batching the per-round copies in fractional sumcheck by increasing the window size from 3 to 4—that would reduce the number of windows from about 9 down to 6, meaning fewer multifolds and M-builds, though there are tradeoffs to consider.\n\nThe M matrix would grow from 64 to 256 entries and the M-build cost could quadruple, so the actual speedup might be around 20ms or could even regress if M becomes too expensive. It's worth exploring since it's just a constant change with low complexity. WHIR is still sitting at 99ms with no optimization work done yet, so I need to dig into what's challenge-dependent versus static there. And I'm looking at the GKR_WINDOW_SIZE constant to see if adjusting it is straightforward.\n\nChanging the window size from 3 to 4 would increase the M matrix to 256 entries, expand the thread block from 64 to 256 threads, and require more shared memory and compute per block. The precompute_m_eval_round_kernel would have more work contracting the larger M, and multifold_kernel would need 16 lookups per element instead of 8. But the tradeoff is fewer windows overall for the inner loop. rounds within each GKR outer round):\n- w=3: ceil(28/3) ≈ 10 windows\n- w=4: ceil(28/4) ≈ 7 windows\n\nSavings from fewer windows:\n- 3 fewer M-builds: saves ~3 × 3ms = ~9ms per call (if M-build stays same time)\n- 3 fewer multifolds: saves ~3 × 3ms = ~9ms per call\n- 3 fewer D2H syncs (one per window): saves ~3 × 0.01ms = negligible\n\nBut the remaining M-builds and multifolds each take MORE time (larger M, more work):\n- M-build with w=4: ~4x more work → maybe 12ms each (vs 3ms)\n- Multifold with w=4: ~2x more work → maybe 6ms each (vs 3ms)\n\nNet: 7 × (12 + 6) = 126ms vs 10 × (3 + 3) = 60ms. WORSE!\n\nThe M-build kernel parallelizes well across tail dimensions with (16,16) blocks handling one (u,v) entry per thread, so increasing w to 4 quadruples the work but doesn't improve parallelization efficiency. The multifold similarly processes 4 variables at once instead of 3, with per-element cost scaling as 2^w, making the larger window size computationally more expensive overall.\n\nActually, wait—the multifold's total work is independent of w because it reads 2^w entries per output element and writes 1, so total reads equal buffer_size regardless of w. With fewer kernel launches, w=4 should be slightly faster despite the higher per-launch cost. Let me recalculate: both w=3 and w=4 do roughly the same total work across all launches, but w=4 saves on launch overhead.\n\nFor M-build, increasing w from 3 to 4 quadruples the work per build (4^4 vs 4^3), but we get 30% fewer builds overall. That nets to about 2.8× more total M-build work, pushing GPU time from ~38ms to ~107ms. For M-eval, the scaling is even worse—w=4 with 4 evals per window means 1024 work units per window versus 192 with w=3 and 3 evals, but we only get 7 windows instead of 10. That's a 3.73× increase in total M-eval work. So expanding the window size actually hurts overall GPU performance significantly. Idea 7 doesn't work.\n\nLet me look at the Openings phase instead. At APC 300 it's 176ms total, but at APC 0 it balloons to 296ms. WHIR scales well (2.21×), but Stacked Reduction stays flat, which is the bottleneck. That's the polynomial opening computation, so I need to understand what's happening there.\n\nThe Stacked Reduction includes NTT reconstruction and batch sumcheck operations. At APC 300 we've already optimized it down to 75ms from a baseline of 311ms, but CPU-bound NTT reconstruction dominates at around 36ms. Attempts to multi-stream this phase actually regressed due to buffer allocation overhead, so there's not much room left to optimize there. WHIR itself is the polynomial commitment scheme using error-correcting codes with NTT, Merkle trees, and interactive algebraic batching rounds.\n\nLooking at the WHIR breakdown at APC 300, the algebraic batch traces kernel takes 18ms across 6 instances, there's Merkle tree construction, batch NTT calls, and a PoW grinding kernel at 14ms across 170 instances. The scaling from APC 0 to APC 300 shows WHIR going from 219ms down to 99ms, which makes sense given the reduced data volume. There's no obvious optimization opportunity there either.\n\nLet me shift focus back to Trace Commit, which is taking 247ms wall time at APC 300. The main contributors are the stack columns kernel at around 16ms and the NTT operations including batch NTT and bit reversal permutation.\n\nI'm wondering if I can pre-compute and cache the NTT expansion that happens during the Reed-Solomon encoding step in Trace Commit. The rs_code_matrix function applies the blowup factor—either 2x or 4x depending on the log_blowup parameter—and this expansion is happening repeatedly. If I can cache this expanded matrix instead of recomputing it each time, that could save meaningful cycles.\n\nLooking at the bottleneck breakdown, I need to prioritize: fractional sumcheck is the biggest win at ~100ms potential savings, followed by trace commit at ~47ms, then MLE rounds at ~33ms. The fractional sumcheck seems like the most promising target to tackle next.\n\nActually, examining the profiling data more closely, I'm noticing something significant—there are over 30,000 host-to-device copies happening across the entire benchmark, totaling 333ms of GPU time. That's a massive amount of transfer overhead. The fractional sumcheck is likely responsible for a chunk of these H2D copies when constructing the eq_buffer, and that's where I should focus my optimization effort.\n\nLooking at the breakdown, the major contributors seem to be the per-AIR weight and rule transfers in round 0, the GKR input evaluations across different AIR types, and the per-round copies during the MLE phase. Each of these categories is generating thousands of individual transfers, which compounds the latency problem.\n\nThe total H2D time is 333ms, but most of those 30K copies are tiny—just a few bytes each—so the real bottleneck is the sheer number of kernel launches and synchronization overhead rather than raw bandwidth. The trace commit phase with stacking, NTT, and Merkle operations adds another few hundred copies on top.\n\nMy best optimization is to batch all the per-AIR round 0 data—weights, equation values, and pointers—into a single consolidated H2D transfer instead of making ~6K separate copies. Round 0 already takes 177ms at APC 300 with 8-thread multi-stream processing, so consolidating these transfers should significantly reduce synchronization overhead. 78 AIRs. For each AIR, the code uploads several small buffers (d_numer_weights, d_denom_weights, d_main_parts pointers, etc.) via individual H2D copies.\n\nAt 623 AIRs × ~5-8 small H2D copies per AIR × 2 segments ≈ 6K-10K individual H2D copies. These are small (typically a few hundred bytes each), but each involves CUDA API overhead.\n\nBy batching all per-AIR data into a single contiguous host-side buffer and doing one H2D copy per segment (or per thread), we'd reduce ~3K H2D copies per thread to ~1. This eliminates the CUDA API overhead for thousands of copies.\n\nThe per-AIR overhead in Round 0 is ~0.18ms per AIR, so 78 AIRs per thread × 0.18ms ≈ 14ms per thread. The measured 177ms total includes ~65ms of GPU kernel execution plus CPU preparation time that can't be overlapped because it runs sequentially on the main thread before spawning the 8 worker threads. Looking at the Round 0 flow, there's CPU preparation for building work items and computing weights, then spawning threads for parallel GPU evaluation, followed by post-processing with a single D2H copy of the batch array and reconstruction.\n\nThe per-segment Round 0 time is ~138ms wall time, with threads taking 93-100ms each. That leaves ~38ms unaccounted for, which is likely the sequential CPU preparation before threads are spawned—things like prepare_work_items, computing per-AIR weights, and building eq_xis. If we could move some of this CPU preparation into the parallel threads or overlap it better, we'd reduce that overhead.\n\nThe weights depend on `xi` from the fractional sumcheck, so we can't pre-compute them at keygen time. But we might be able to parallelize the weight computation itself using rayon if the calculations for different AIRs are independent. Looking at the code structure, I'm examining how the weight computation loop iterates through traces and interactions to see if we can split that work across threads. The weight computation itself is quite fast—around 1.3 million operations total across all interactions, which takes roughly a millisecond on a single core. So that's not where the bottleneck is. Let me trace through what else happens during sequential preparation before the threads start. The `prepare_work_items` function builds the work structs and pre-computes buffer sizes to avoid per-AIR overhead, so I need to see what's actually consuming time in the CPU preparation phase.\n\nI keep circling back to the same analysis without making progress. Let me commit to a concrete direction: parallelizing the sequential CPU preparation in Round 0 using rayon. Though I should note that a previous attempt to parallelize post-processing (transpose and iDFT) only yielded 10ms improvement because the real bottleneck was D2H copy latency, not compute. But pre-processing is different—it's the weight computation and work item preparation before threads spawn. Even so, I've shown those are both quite fast individually. The ~38ms gap must be coming from somewhere else.\n\nThe real culprits are probably thread spawning overhead, initial GPU buffer allocations per thread, and the cold GPU pipeline on first kernel launches. Those are structural issues that won't budge without major refactoring.\n\nLet me try something completely different. I'm looking at the trace commit path and noticing that we're doing an NTT expansion to move the trace polynomial into the evaluation domain. But in many STARK systems, the trace is already generated as evaluations, so this recomputation might be redundant.\n\nThe stacked trace goes through stacking, then NTT expansion (which doubles the height if blowup is 2), then Merkle hashing. That NTT operates on the full matrix—106K columns times the row count. I'm wondering if we could hash rows incrementally during the NTT or switch to a commitment scheme that skips the full expansion entirely.\n\nActually, this is getting too speculative. I need to find something concrete and implementable.\n\nLooking at the fractional sumcheck's eq_buffer construction per GKR round—I keep dismissing it as only ~6ms, but what if the actual overhead is larger? At round 28, the SqrtEqLayers::from_xi call with a 27-element xi_prev builds 13 low layers and 14 high layers, with the largest reaching 16K elements. The eq_hypercube kernel on that size is small, dominated by launch overhead, but running 14 of these layers adds up.\n\nCounting the kernel launches, allocations, and host-to-device transfers across all 28 rounds, I'm looking at roughly 1.9ms just from the kernels, and with allocation overhead that balloons to 6-8ms total. So it's genuinely small.\n\nI need to step back and look for a different angle. Let me examine what optimizations have actually paid off before—the batch-stacked-reduction-mle-sync saved 192ms by eliminating per-window D2H syncs, and multistream-round0-parallel gained 313ms by running 8 concurrent streams for Round 0. I should study these patterns more carefully to find where the next big win might be. stream-gkr-input-eval: 223ms (8 concurrent streams for GKR input)\n4. batch-stacking-scatter-kernel: 149ms (batched scatter for trace stacking)\n5. increase-multistream-thread-count: 61ms (4→8 threads)\n6. prealloc-gkr-input-buffers: 217ms (eliminated per-AIR mutex contention)\n7. batch-mle-round-kernels: ~60ms openings improvement\n8. prealloc-round0-threshold-buffers: 35ms (95th-percentile pre-allocation)\n9. round0-interleaved-work-balance: 22ms (round-robin vs contiguous chunks)\n10. gpu-round0-poly-extraction: 47ms (GPU-side matrix-vector multiply)\n11. logup-precompute-round0: 64ms (background thread for precompute)\n12. batch-mle-interpolation: 23ms (batched descriptor-array interpolation)\n\nThe pattern's clear—the biggest gains came from removing synchronization bottlenecks, adding multi-streaming where possible, and eliminating per-AIR overhead. The remaining opportunities at this scale would need either new synchronization points to eliminate, additional multi-streaming windows, or more per-AIR overhead to uncover. Multi-streaming's already applied to Round 0 and GKR input evaluation, so the main sequential phase left is Trace Commit.\n\nLooking at what's still single-stream—fractional sumcheck, MLE rounds, and openings—I'm wondering if Trace Commit could benefit from multi-streaming if the hash kernels aren't fully saturating the GPU, though past experience suggests large kernels do saturate it. The remaining synchronization points are mostly per-round D2H transfers in fractional sumcheck and MLE rounds, but those are inherent to the protocol since we need CPU-side Fiat-Shamir. The only real way around that would be GPU-side transcript handling. I'm also considering whether there's a fundamentally different approach to the fractional sumcheck itself—the protocol currently has O(n) GKR rounds with O(n) inner sumcheck rounds each, and I'm wondering if there's a way to restructure that.\n\nThe precompute-M optimization already reduces D2H syncs by a window factor w, so with w=3 we go from O(n²) syncs down to O(n²/w). I'm thinking about whether we could apply similar windowing to the outer GKR rounds themselves—batching multiple GKR rounds together to eliminate D2H syncs between them.\n\nBut there's a fundamental dependency issue: each outer round's claims depend on the previous round's tree revert plus the first sumcheck result. The tree revert is a GPU operation that must complete before the inner sumcheck can start, and the inner sumcheck requires D2H for each round. So we can't really skip the outer round boundaries without breaking the data dependencies.\n\nUnless... what if we could batch the tree reverts and first sumcheck rounds from multiple outer rounds into a single kernel? If we could compute s'(1) and s'(2) for multiple layers simultaneously in one kernel launch, we might reduce the sync overhead. But the layers have sequential dependencies—each layer's tree revert depends on the previous layer's result—so batching them together seems impossible without breaking the computation order.\n\nLet me think about reducing the number of GKR rounds instead. The round count is determined by n_logup = log2_ceil(total_interactions + 1) - l_skip. At APC 300, with total_interactions around 448M, that gives us roughly 29 rounds. To reduce this, we'd need to either increase l_skip (skip more lower bits) or reduce total_interactions, but both of those are constrained by the protocol and circuit design. I'm checking what l_skip is currently set to in the code.\n\nIncreasing l_skip would cut down the GKR rounds but pack more interactions into each leaf, increasing per-leaf computation. Since this is a protocol-level parameter and the guidelines say not to change the underlying cryptographic protocol, this approach is off the table.\n\nSo I'm back to finding a concrete bottleneck with a clear implementation path. Let me review the 25 optimization tasks and see which ones still have potential—looking at what's succeeded so far versus what's failed. round-robin, rightsize)\n- Stacked Reduction: 3 tasks (1 success: batch-mle-sync; 1 success: batch-mle-round; 1 failure: multistream)\n- Trace Commit: 1 task (success: batch-stacking-scatter)\n- MLE Rounds: 3 tasks (1 success: batch-interpolation; 2 failures: batch-main-ptrs, bulk-alloc)\n- LogUp GKR overall: 3 tasks (1 success: overlap-logup; 2 failures: batch-scatter-gkr, warp-tiled)\n- Thread count: 1 task (success: increase-multistream)\n- Keygen precompute: 1 task (failure: precompute-logup-round0-rules)\n- Batch Round 0: 2 failures (descriptor-arrays, caller-routing)\n\nI'm looking at what hasn't been explored yet: multi-streaming the Trace Commit phase, GPU-side Fiat-Shamir transcripts, expanding the GKR window, fusing MLE kernels, overlapping phases where the protocol allows, reducing NTT costs, caching Merkle trees across segments, and cutting down on D2H copies in fractional sumcheck. One thing that stands out is whether I can overlap the WHIR opening computation across segments—since there's one WHIR invocation per segment and with 2 segments they might be running sequentially, there could be parallelization potential there.\n\nActually, thinking through the dependencies more carefully: the WHIR opening for each segment depends on the random challenge from the verifier, which only comes after processing the previous segment's opening queries. So they have to run sequentially.\n\nLet me focus instead on optimizing the Trace Commit phase itself, specifically the row hashing step. The current approach uses Poseidon2 to hash all columns in each row, which at 106K columns and APC 300 means roughly 6.6K to 13K sponge absorptions per row depending on the rate parameter. Each absorption is a full Poseidon2 permutation, so there's real overhead here. If I could restructure how columns are processed—maybe batching multiple rows or using a tree-based approach within each row—I might be able to reduce the total number of absorptions needed.\n\nBut wait, that would fundamentally change the hash function behavior, which isn't allowed since it's part of the protocol.\n\nWhat if I use multiple GPU streams to parallelize the Trace Commits across different segments? At APC 300 there are 2 segments, and each segment's trace commit is independent. Running them concurrently on