{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014oNmZUwDBKV7XhTaZA2DCx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Multi-stream Stacked Reduction Round 0 and PLE Fold\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tMulti-stream the per-trace sequential kernel loops in Stacked Reduction's `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` and `fold_ple_evals`. These functions launch ~797 sequential small CUDA kernels on a single stream at APC 300 (~39ms GPU time). The optimization distributes traces across 8 OS threads with per-thread `cudaStreamPerThread` — the same pattern that improved Round 0 constraint eval by 1.89x and GKR input eval by 1.40x — to enable concurrent kernel execution and reduce wall time.\n6\t\n7\t## Implementation\n8\t\n9\t### Files modified:\n10\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` — Main implementation\n11\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` — Made `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `_stacked_reduction_fold_ple` FFI functions public for direct use from worker threads\n12\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/error.rs` — Added `CurrentStreamSync` variant to `StackedReductionError`\n13\t\n14\t### Key changes:\n15\t\n16\t**`batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`**: Pre-computed per-trace work items (trace_ptr, height, width, lambda_offset, n_value), sorted by descending height. For >= 100 traces, pre-allocated per-thread `d_g_pos`, `d_g_neg[0..l_skip]`, and `d_block_sums` buffers (8 threads × 14 buffers = 112 DeviceBuffers). Dispatched work items round-robin across threads. After threads join, reduced per-thread accumulators on CPU (D2H copy + element-wise addition) and uploaded the sums back to device for `reconstruct_s0_from_g`.\n17\t\n18\t**`fold_ple_evals`**: Pre-computed per-trace `dst_offset` values (the running sum of folded heights × widths). For >= 100 traces per stacked_per_commit group, dispatched traces across 8 threads. Each thread writes to non-overlapping regions of the shared `folded_evals` buffer (no per-thread accumulation needed). Called `current_stream_sync()` after `fill_zero` to ensure visibility to worker streams.\n19\t\n20\t**Deviation from plan**: Used `usize` pointer casts for thread safety instead of `SendPtr` wrapper, as the wrapper approach still triggered Rust's `Send` checker on raw pointers inside closures.\n21\t\n22\t## Results\n23\t\n24\t### APC 300\n25\t\n26\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n27\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n28\t| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1382ms | 1558ms | -897ms, 1.58x lower | +176ms, 1.13x higher |\n29\t| Constraints | - | 947ms | 1134ms | - | +187ms, 1.20x higher |\n30\t| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 531ms | 724ms | -66ms, 1.09x lower | +193ms, 1.36x higher |\n31\t| Round 0 | 662ms | 242ms | 238ms | -424ms, 2.78x lower | -4ms, unchanged |\n32\t| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 172ms | 170ms | -10ms, 1.06x lower | -2ms, unchanged |\n33\t| Openings | 413ms | 176ms | 175ms | -238ms, 2.36x lower | -1ms, unchanged |\n34\t| Stacked Reduction | 311ms | 75ms | 74ms | -237ms, 4.20x lower | -1ms, unchanged |\n35\t| Trace Commit | 406ms | 256ms | 247ms | -159ms, 1.64x lower | -9ms, noise |\n36\t\n37\t### APC 100\n38\t\n39\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n40\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n41\t| STARK excl trace | 2155ms | 1745ms | 1979ms | -176ms, 1.09x lower | +234ms, 1.13x higher |\n42\t| LogUp GKR | 775ms | 858ms | 1091ms | +316ms, 1.41x higher | +233ms, 1.27x higher |\n43\t| Stacked Reduction | 202ms | 68ms | 70ms | -132ms, 2.89x lower | +2ms, unchanged |\n44\t\n45\t### APC 0\n46\t\n47\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n48\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n49\t| STARK excl trace | 2153ms | 2145ms | 2172ms | +19ms, unchanged | +27ms, noise |\n50\t| LogUp GKR | 993ms | 1016ms | 1040ms | +47ms, noise | +24ms, noise |\n51\t| Stacked Reduction | 113ms | 76ms | 76ms | -37ms, 1.49x lower | 0ms, unchanged |\n52\t\n53\t## Assessment\n54\t\n55\t**Failed.** The optimization did not improve Stacked Reduction performance and caused severe cross-phase regression in LogUp GKR.\n56\t\n57\t**Stacked Reduction itself barely changed**: 75ms → 74ms at APC 300 (-1ms), far below the 15ms rollback threshold. The expected ~31ms savings from concurrent kernel execution did not materialize.\n58\t\n59\t**LogUp GKR regressed significantly**: +193ms at APC 300, +233ms at APC 100. No regression at APC 0 (sequential fallback, < 100 traces). This confirms the regression is from the multi-stream runtime behavior, not binary codegen changes.\n60\t\n61\t**Root cause analysis**:\n62\t\n63\t1. **Buffer allocation overhead dominates savings**: The multi-stream path pre-allocates 112 DeviceBuffers (8 threads × 14 buffers: 1 d_g_pos + 12 d_g_neg + 1 d_block_sums per thread). Each allocation goes through the CUDA memory pool via `cudaMallocAsync`. While individual allocations are fast (~5μs each), 112 allocations add ~1ms overhead, and the subsequent deallocation through `cudaFreeAsync` (112 calls on thread scope exit) disrupts the CUDA memory pool state.\n64\t\n65\t2. **Cross-phase memory pool degradation**: The 112 buffer allocations+frees in Segment 0's Stacked Reduction phase alter the CUDA memory pool's internal state (fragmentation, free-list structure). When Segment 1's LogUp GKR subsequently allocates its working buffers, the pool serves them from different physical locations or requires more coalescing, degrading memory access patterns. This explains why GKR (memory-bandwidth-bound) regresses while compute-bound phases (Round 0, MLE) are unaffected.\n66\t\n67\t3. **CPU reconstruction dominates Stacked Reduction**: The 75ms Stacked Reduction total includes ~39ms GPU kernel time and ~36ms CPU NTT reconstruction. Even eliminating all GPU time would only reduce total to ~36ms. The multi-stream GPU savings (~31ms) are partially offset by D2H reduction overhead (~11ms: 10MB D2H at 12GB/s + 10M field additions on CPU).\n68\t\n69\t4. **fold_ple multi-stream ineffective**: The fold_ple phase contributes only ~9ms GPU time. Multi-streaming would save at most 7ms, but the per-group trace count threshold (>= 100) means only the common_main group activates multi-threading.\n70\t\n71\t## Future Work\n72\t\n73\t- **Avoid per-thread DeviceBuffer allocations**: The multi-stream pattern works well for Round 0 and GKR input eval because those phases don't need per-thread accumulation buffers. Stacked Reduction's `final_reduce_block_sums<true>` uses non-atomic `+=`, requiring per-thread output buffers. Switching to `atomicAdd` in the CUDA kernel would eliminate all per-thread buffers, making multi-streaming feasible without memory pool disruption.\n74\t\n75\t- **Reduce CPU reconstruction time**: The NTT-based polynomial multiplication in `reconstruct_s0_from_g` takes ~36ms on CPU. Moving this to GPU (batched NTT on device) would reduce the CPU bottleneck that limits multi-stream GPU savings.\n76\t\n77\t- **Memory pool warming**: Allocating and immediately freeing scratch buffers before the critical GKR phase could stabilize the pool state. This is fragile and environment-dependent.\n78\t\n79\t- **Kernel fusion**: Instead of multi-streaming many small kernels, fuse the per-trace loop into a single batched kernel (similar to the MLE round batching). This avoids threading overhead entirely and could reduce the ~39ms GPU time to <5ms with a single kernel launch.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"c17389b2-d18c-4383-a9ca-d204574b6163","timestamp":"2026-04-14T14:29:16.761Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/report.md","content":"# Report: Multi-stream Stacked Reduction Round 0 and PLE Fold\n\n## Description\n\nMulti-stream the per-trace sequential kernel loops in Stacked Reduction's `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` and `fold_ple_evals`. These functions launch ~797 sequential small CUDA kernels on a single stream at APC 300 (~39ms GPU time). The optimization distributes traces across 8 OS threads with per-thread `cudaStreamPerThread` — the same pattern that improved Round 0 constraint eval by 1.89x and GKR input eval by 1.40x — to enable concurrent kernel execution and reduce wall time.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Files modified:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs` — Main implementation\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` — Made `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `_stacked_reduction_fold_ple` FFI functions public for direct use from worker threads\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/error.rs` — Added `CurrentStreamSync` variant to `StackedReductionError`\n\n### Key changes:\n\n**`batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`**: Pre-computed per-trace work items (trace_ptr, height, width, lambda_offset, n_value), sorted by descending height. For >= 100 traces, pre-allocated per-thread `d_g_pos`, `d_g_neg[0..l_skip]`, and `d_block_sums` buffers (8 threads × 14 buffers = 112 DeviceBuffers). Dispatched work items round-robin across threads. After threads join, reduced per-thread accumulators on CPU (D2H copy + element-wise addition) and uploaded the sums back to device for `reconstruct_s0_from_g`.\n\n**`fold_ple_evals`**: Pre-computed per-trace `dst_offset` values (the running sum of folded heights × widths). For >= 100 traces per stacked_per_commit group, dispatched traces across 8 threads. Each thread writes to non-overlapping regions of the shared `folded_evals` buffer (no per-thread accumulation needed). Called `current_stream_sync()` after `fill_zero` to ensure visibility to worker streams.\n\n**Deviation from plan**: Used `usize` pointer casts for thread safety instead of `SendPtr` wrapper, as the wrapper approach still triggered Rust's `Send` checker on raw pointers inside closures.\n\n## Results\n\n### APC 300\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1382ms | 1558ms | -897ms, 1.58x lower | +176ms, 1.13x higher |\n| Constraints | - | 947ms | 1134ms | - | +187ms, 1.20x higher |\n| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 531ms | 724ms | -66ms, 1.09x lower | +193ms, 1.36x higher |\n| Round 0 | 662ms | 242ms | 238ms | -424ms, 2.78x lower | -4ms, unchanged |\n| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 172ms | 170ms | -10ms, 1.06x lower | -2ms, unchanged |\n| Openings | 413ms | 176ms | 175ms | -238ms, 2.36x lower | -1ms, unchanged |\n| Stacked Reduction | 311ms | 75ms | 74ms | -237ms, 4.20x lower | -1ms, unchanged |\n| Trace Commit | 406ms | 256ms | 247ms | -159ms, 1.64x lower | -9ms, noise |\n\n### APC 100\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2155ms | 1745ms | 1979ms | -176ms, 1.09x lower | +234ms, 1.13x higher |\n| LogUp GKR | 775ms | 858ms | 1091ms | +316ms, 1.41x higher | +233ms, 1.27x higher |\n| Stacked Reduction | 202ms | 68ms | 70ms | -132ms, 2.89x lower | +2ms, unchanged |\n\n### APC 0\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2153ms | 2145ms | 2172ms | +19ms, unchanged | +27ms, noise |\n| LogUp GKR | 993ms | 1016ms | 1040ms | +47ms, noise | +24ms, noise |\n| Stacked Reduction | 113ms | 76ms | 76ms | -37ms, 1.49x lower | 0ms, unchanged |\n\n## Assessment\n\n**Failed.** The optimization did not improve Stacked Reduction performance and caused severe cross-phase regression in LogUp GKR.\n\n**Stacked Reduction itself barely changed**: 75ms → 74ms at APC 300 (-1ms), far below the 15ms rollback threshold. The expected ~31ms savings from concurrent kernel execution did not materialize.\n\n**LogUp GKR regressed significantly**: +193ms at APC 300, +233ms at APC 100. No regression at APC 0 (sequential fallback, < 100 traces). This confirms the regression is from the multi-stream runtime behavior, not binary codegen changes.\n\n**Root cause analysis**:\n\n1. **Buffer allocation overhead dominates savings**: The multi-stream path pre-allocates 112 DeviceBuffers (8 threads × 14 buffers: 1 d_g_pos + 12 d_g_neg + 1 d_block_sums per thread). Each allocation goes through the CUDA memory pool via `cudaMallocAsync`. While individual allocations are fast (~5μs each), 112 allocations add ~1ms overhead, and the subsequent deallocation through `cudaFreeAsync` (112 calls on thread scope exit) disrupts the CUDA memory pool state.\n\n2. **Cross-phase memory pool degradation**: The 112 buffer allocations+frees in Segment 0's Stacked Reduction phase alter the CUDA memory pool's internal state (fragmentation, free-list structure). When Segment 1's LogUp GKR subsequently allocates its working buffers, the pool serves them from different physical locations or requires more coalescing, degrading memory access patterns. This explains why GKR (memory-bandwidth-bound) regresses while compute-bound phases (Round 0, MLE) are unaffected.\n\n3. **CPU reconstruction dominates Stacked Reduction**: The 75ms Stacked Reduction total includes ~39ms GPU kernel time and ~36ms CPU NTT reconstruction. Even eliminating all GPU time would only reduce total to ~36ms. The multi-stream GPU savings (~31ms) are partially offset by D2H reduction overhead (~11ms: 10MB D2H at 12GB/s + 10M field additions on CPU).\n\n4. **fold_ple multi-stream ineffective**: The fold_ple phase contributes only ~9ms GPU time. Multi-streaming would save at most 7ms, but the per-group trace count threshold (>= 100) means only the common_main group activates multi-threading.\n\n## Future Work\n\n- **Avoid per-thread DeviceBuffer allocations**: The multi-stream pattern works well for Round 0 and GKR input eval because those phases don't need per-thread accumulation buffers. Stacked Reduction's `final_reduce_block_sums<true>` uses non-atomic `+=`, requiring per-thread output buffers. Switching to `atomicAdd` in the CUDA kernel would eliminate all per-thread buffers, making multi-streaming feasible without memory pool disruption.\n\n- **Reduce CPU reconstruction time**: The NTT-based polynomial multiplication in `reconstruct_s0_from_g` takes ~36ms on CPU. Moving this to GPU (batched NTT on device) would reduce the CPU bottleneck that limits multi-stream GPU savings.\n\n- **Memory pool warming**: Allocating and immediately freeing scratch buffers before the critical GKR phase could stabilize the pool state. This is fragile and environment-dependent.\n\n- **Kernel fusion**: Instead of multi-streaming many small kernels, fuse the per-trace loop into a single batched kernel (similar to the MLE round batching). This avoids threading overhead entirely and could reduce the ~39ms GPU time to <5ms with a single kernel launch.\n","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":80}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Jm1bY9VDb856yfcPZM1thF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so the report confirms:\n1. Stacked Reduction is 75ms total = ~39ms GPU kernel time + ~36ms CPU NTT reconstruction\n2. The CPU NTT reconstruction in `reconstruct_s0_from_g` takes ~36ms\n\nThis is a concrete, significant bottleneck. If we could move the CPU NTT reconstruction to GPU, we'd save ~30-35ms (most of the 36ms).\n\nBut wait, the NTT sizes are small (l_skip is typically 3-7, so domain sizes are 8-128). GPU NTT on such small sizes would be faster than CPU (especially batched), but the savings might be limited since the CPU NTT on small sizes is already fast per call.\n\n\nActually, 36ms for NTT on small polynomials seems off. Let me think about what's really happening in `reconstruct_s0_from_g` — it's doing inverse DFT on G evaluations, then DFT/iDFT for polynomial multiplication, and this repeats across multiple g_pos and g_neg groups. The domain sizes are tied to the stacking layout's height, not just l_skip, so the actual polynomial sizes might be much larger than I initially thought.\n\nLooking at the `ntt_multiply_and_add` function, the `domain_size` parameter is set to `large_uni_domain`, which is calculated as 2 × skip_domain × neg_count.next_power_of_two(). So for l_skip=7 with 4 negative groups, that's 1024, but for l_skip=3 with 32 groups, it drops to 512 — the domain size varies significantly depending on the skip and group configuration.\n\nThe 36ms total NTT time seems high for these domain sizes since a CPU NTT on 1K elements should take under 0.1ms. The issue is likely that `reconstruct_s0_from_g` gets called once per segment across all stacked commits, and each call triggers multiple NTTs. But the 36ms might also include the D2H copy of G evaluations (which could be several MB), CPU polynomial multiplications, memory allocations, and other overhead. Looking at the round 0 flow, the GPU kernel computing G0, G1, G2 takes about 39ms, followed by a D2H copy of maybe 5ms, then the CPU reconstruction work — so the bottleneck might not be pure NTT computation but the full pipeline including data movement and intermediate allocations.\n\nActually, the report mentions 39ms GPU kernel time plus 36ms CPU NTT reconstruction totaling 75ms, but the nsys data shows 797 kernel instances of `stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel` with only 22ms total GPU time, which works out to about 28μs per kernel. That 22ms doesn't match the 39ms quoted, so the 39ms likely includes additional kernels beyond just the block sum kernel.\n\nThe real bottleneck I'm seeing is that 36ms of CPU NTT reconstruction happening in stacked reduction round 0. After the MLE sync and batch-mle-round-kernels optimizations, the MLE rounds themselves are fast at around 2ms, but round 0 is still eating up 75ms total. WHIR is also significant at 99ms for the openings component.\n\nThe question is whether I can move that CPU polynomial multiplication work (NTT plus pointwise multiply) onto the GPU instead.  I could batch all three polynomial pairs into a single GPU NTT call, do the pointwise multiplication on GPU, then batch the inverse NTT—the domain sizes are small enough that this would only take about 0.1ms. But looking deeper, that 36ms probably isn't just the NTT computation itself. It's likely a combination of copying G evaluations from GPU to host memory, the actual polynomial operations on CPU, the NTT-based multiplication, and various memory allocations and transfers. If I kept everything on GPU, I'd eliminate those data movement costs entirely.\n\nThe optimization is straightforward: since the G evaluations are already on GPU from the previous kernel, I can do the polynomial multiplication right there using the existing batch NTT infrastructure, then only copy back the final s_0 coefficients. That saves the expensive D2H transfer of the full G evaluations and avoids the CPU polynomial operations entirely—potentially saving 25-35ms. The operations themselves are all feasible with the existing GPU NTT kernel, which can handle these small domain sizes. This is my selected optimization! Though 30ms falls short of my 50ms target, so I'm wondering if there's a complementary approach. Actually, I realize the 36ms CPU NTT time isn't the only bottleneck—keeping G on GPU also avoids a ~10MB D2H transfer that probably adds another 5-10ms. So the real savings would be closer to 39-44ms total when accounting for the GPU NTT overhead.\n\nNow I'm checking whether this meets the improvement threshold from previous work. A 40ms gain on the 75ms Stacked Reduction component would cut it nearly in half, but at the full STARK trace level of 1302ms, that's only 3%—right at the edge of the 5% cutoff. Looking back at what actually shipped though, several smaller improvements in the 20-35ms range were kept, so this seems justified.\n\nI'm going ahead with the optimization. Let me scan the stacked reduction code to see if there's other work we could move to GPU alongside this. The fold_ple_evals, MLE rounds, and round 0 kernel are already GPU-accelerated, leaving only the reconstruction on CPU. I'm also noticing the code multiplies polynomials using NTT for the E_neg values...\n\nExamining polynomial operations...\n\nThose NTT multiplications are CPU-bound too. So the full strategy is to keep G evaluations on GPU, upload the E polynomials, run all the polynomial multiplications on GPU, and only transfer back the final s_0 coefficients. This gives us a complete GPU-ification of the round 0 reconstruction phase. I'm satisfied with this approach and ready to implement it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3251,"cache_read_input_tokens":164947,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3251},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"stand