CPU rule/DAG computation (~0.05ms based on earlier findings), H2D uploads (~0.1ms for small copies), and kernel launch (~0.01ms), totaling around 0.16ms per AIR. Across 791 AIRs and 8 threads, that's only ~16ms of overhead—much lower than the 1.8ms per AIR I was concerned about.\n\nFor seg0's GKR input eval taking 241ms total, I need to account for how multi-streaming actually affects wall time. The GPU kernel time for GLOBAL-mode AIRs is probably around 300ms of the 439ms total (since seg0 has larger AIRs), but with 8 streams running on 128 SMs, each kernel using 256 blocks can only achieve about 2.5x concurrent execution. So the wall time would be roughly 300ms divided by 2.5, giving ~120ms, plus the ~16ms per-AIR overhead.\n\nBut the measured time is 241ms, leaving a ~100ms gap. I'm realizing the issue might be that the GKR input eval kernel is memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound. If that's the case, running multiple instances concurrently doesn't actually help—they just share the same bandwidth bottleneck, so the total throughput stays the same whether they run sequentially or in parallel.\n\nYet the multi-streaming did improve performance by 1.40x, which suggests the benefit comes from overlapping CPU overhead and per-AIR work with GPU execution, rather than from true parallel GPU throughput. The 241ms for seg0 is likely the serialized GPU time plus CPU overhead, and the 439ms total for GLOBAL GKR input eval is split between seg0 and seg1.\n\nLooking at seg0 more closely, if it has ~200ms of GPU time and 300 AIRs, the per-AIR CPU overhead of ~48ms should compress to ~6ms wall time when overlapped across 8 threads, plus ~5ms for synchronization, giving roughly 211ms total — but we're measuring 241ms, so there's a ~30ms gap. That difference likely comes from GPU context switching between streams, CUDA memory pool contention, or CPU work that happens before the threads even spawn, like collecting interaction data, building work items, sorting by height, and computing max values.\n\nThis pre-spawn work is sequential and could easily take 20-30ms. Since the proving key and context don't change between segments, we could cache the work item metadata at keygen time and only update the trace pointers per segment, saving that 10-20ms of overhead. It's a reasonable optimization with medium-high probability of working and moderate complexity — I just need to verify the actual magnitude by looking at the code.\n\nLooking at the work item construction in the GKR input code, each AIR involves looking up interaction metadata from the proving key and building the work item struct with pointer copies and interaction rules — lightweight work at about 0.01ms per AIR, so roughly 8ms across 791 AIRs, plus a few more milliseconds for sorting and buffer size computation. That puts us around 10-12ms total for this pre-spawn phase.\n\nI keep circling back to the same optimization: pre-allocating output buffers for the constraints-phase MLE fold using ping-pong buffers to eliminate those 37K DeviceBuffer allocations per benchmark. But I already know this was attempted in the bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers task and reverted because the improvement was only 13ms — below the rollback threshold. The CUDA pool allocator's per-call overhead is already quite small at 0.3-0.5μs, so there's a hard limit on how much we can save here.\n\nLet me pivot to the D2H transfers in the constraints MLE rounds instead. We're doing 40 D2H syncs across 20 rounds with 2 segments each, and the bottleneck isn't the actual data transfer of those ~100 bytes — it's the GPU kernel completion wait time. Each kernel takes about 2.5ms to finish, and the stream sync is what forces us to wait for that before we can proceed.\n\nThe real problem is the GPU sitting idle between rounds. After each kernel finishes and we copy the data, the CPU has to process the transcript before launching the next kernel, which creates gaps of roughly 0.1-0.5ms per round. Across 40 rounds, that's around 12ms of wasted GPU time that we could theoretically recover if we kept the GPU busy during transcript processing.\n\nBut we can't speculatively launch the next kernel because the random challenge for the fold is unpredictable — it's a hash output that depends on the transcript we just processed.\n\nI'm going to try a different angle entirely. What if we built a persistent kernel that handles all the constraints-phase MLE rounds without needing CPU synchronization between them?\n\nThe kernel would stay resident on the GPU and internally loop through each round: compute the fold using the previous challenge, evaluate the batch polynomials, write the results to a pinned buffer asynchronously, then wait for the CPU to finish processing the transcript and write the new challenge back via a memory-mapped flag. Once the challenge is ready, the kernel reads it and moves to the next round.\n\nThis eliminates kernel launch overhead, GPU idle time, and per-round buffer management — roughly 24ms of savings total.\n\nThe tradeoff is that this approach is complex: it requires a new persistent CUDA kernel, careful GPU-host signaling, and flag-based synchronization. The medium probability of success and high complexity don't justify the modest 24ms gain.\n\nLet me step back and focus on the actual bottleneck. The MLE rounds are taking 173ms at APC 300, which breaks down to about 4.3ms per round across 40 rounds and 2 segments — roughly 2.5ms of GPU kernel time and 1.8ms of non-GPU overhead per round.\n\nThat overhead is split across CPU descriptor building, device-to-host transfers, GPU fold operations, and buffer management. The fold operation itself is the real culprit: allocating 37K buffers, uploading pointer arrays, launching the kernel, and freeing those buffers adds up to about 0.6ms per round. If I could use a single pre-allocated buffer with ping-pong swapping instead, I'd save roughly 24ms across all rounds.\n\nThe bulk-alloc task attempted this but only recovered 13