down, the GPU kernel itself is only ~0.2ms, but there's about 1.0ms of non-GPU overhead per round. That overhead breaks down into descriptor building and metadata (~0.3ms), D2H transfers and transcript work (~0.2ms), and the fold_mle_evals function (~0.5ms). The fold_mle_evals is doing a lot of per-trace DeviceBuffer allocations—around 800 allocs per round for the q_evals alone, plus frees for the old ones, plus a couple more buffers for eq_r_ns and k_rot_ns. Those 800 allocations are eating up about 0.32ms per round just on their own.\n\nIf I can eliminate these per-round allocations by switching to pre-allocated ping-pong buffers, I'd save roughly 26ms across the full 40 rounds. But when I look at the CONSTRAINTS MLE rounds with a similar scale of ~37K allocations, the actual savings were only 13ms, which suggests the CUDA pool allocation overhead might be closer to 0.3μs rather than 0.4μs, or some allocations are getting cached and become nearly free. The arena approach itself probably adds some overhead too.\n\nFor the stacked reduction specifically, I'm estimating around 19.2ms total from the 32K allocations and 32K frees, though the calculation seems to cut off there.\n\nLooking at the actual allocation pattern for the stacked reduction fold, each DeviceBuffer gets a size based on the trace dimensions at that reduction level—early rounds have large buffers while later rounds have tiny ones. The CUDA pool allocator is quite efficient with small allocations from the free list, but large allocations might be slower. A bulk-alloc approach using a single contiguous arena per round instead of 800 individual allocations would avoid pool fragmentation and reduce overhead.\n\nThis could save around 15ms from the 47ms total in the MLE rounds—about a 32% reduction. Combined with GPU polynomial reconstruction for round 0, that's roughly 20ms total savings, but the complexity is substantial and a similar approach was already reverted for the constraints MLE. Let me step back and focus on the simplest path forward: GPU-side polynomial reconstruction for stacked reduction round 0 plus pre-allocation for the MLE rounds. Actually, the polynomial reconstruction alone only saves 5ms, so that's not compelling enough. Looking at the bigger picture, the real bottleneck is those 32K allocations and frees happening in the stacked reduction MLE rounds.\n\nBut wait—I'm realizing the constraints MLE rounds have the same pattern with 37K allocations costing about 15ms. If I apply the same pre-allocated ping-pong buffer approach to both phases simultaneously, I could save around 28ms total (13ms from constraints plus 15ms from stacked). That's actually above the 20ms threshold that caused the previous rollback, even though the constraints bulk-alloc alone was reverted.\n\nLooking back at the task history, the batch-mle-interpolation task succeeded with just 23ms improvement, so 28ms would be competitive. The real advantage here is that I'd be targeting the stacked reduction MLE fold specifically—a different code path than what was already tried—and the stacked reduction implementation is simpler without needing the MatrixRef abstraction.\n\nThe overhead is proportionally much larger in this path: 15ms out of 47ms total is 32% versus only 9% in the broader context. But even with that relative advantage, the absolute improvement of ~15ms translates to just 1.2% at the STARK exclusion trace level, which is smaller than previous accepted optimizations.\n\nLet me step back and look at where the real bottlenecks are. The GKR input evaluation on segment 0 is taking 241ms, and the fractional sumcheck is another 219ms—these are the two biggest costs I should be focusing on.\n\nBreaking down the fractional sumcheck: the tree building takes about 30ms per segment, while the actual sumcheck rounds consume roughly 80ms per segment across GPU kernel execution and CPU overhead. With around 29 total rounds and the precompute-M strategy using a window size of 3, eac