seg0, splitting them across 4 streams could cut the per-AIR time roughly in half—saving around 100ms total if two launches can overlap. The approach is mechanically straightforward and should work correctly.\n\nThe real win here is restructuring the host-side scheduling: instead of assigning whole AIRs to threads (which leaves smaller threads idle while large ones block), I'd split large AIRs into tile-range sub-tasks and distribute those across threads for better load balancing. This means adding tile offset and count parameters to the kernel launcher and computing tile ranges in the host loop.\n\nBut there's a wrinkle with the intermediates buffer—each thread has its own pre-allocated buffer in GLOBAL mode, so I need to make sure the per-AIR kernel parameters (preprocessed data, rules, etc.) are accessible to all sub-tasks of the same AIR across different threads. I'm thinking of pre-building...\n\nThe work item already stores references to the AIR context and public key, so I just need to extend it with tile range information so each sub-task knows which tiles it's responsible for. That should handle the parameter sharing cleanly. Let me write this out as the final optimization.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1780,"cache_read_input_tokens":216524,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1780},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"4ee301ac-54b7-4864-bfa7-ae20de3dc640"}
