{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XmPmS33SGgHrAxczU5huja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the full picture. The CUDA launcher at line 216 launches the kernel with:\n- For GLOBAL: count = TASK_SIZE (65536), grid based on TASK_SIZE/256 = 256 blocks\n- `num_rows_per_tile` is passed to the kernel, which loops: `for (j=0; j < num_rows_per_tile; j++)`\n\nSo ONE kernel launch processes ALL tiles for one AIR. The kernel has an internal tile loop.\n\nTo split this, I need to:\n1. Add `tile_offset` parameter to the kernel and launcher\n2. The kernel loop becomes: `for (j=tile_offset; j < tile_offset+num_rows_per_tile; j++)`\n\n\n3. The host will invoke the kernel multiple times with different tile offsets and row counts for each sub-task.\n\nFor the output writes, since `out_idx` depends on the row index which varies with each tile, different sub-tasks naturally write to different output positions without conflicts. The intermediates buffer is thread-local for each sub-task, so there's no contention there either—each sub-task accesses its own intermediate storage indexed by the source's index.\n\nIn SCATTER mode, intermediates live in registers, so splitting across multiple kernel launches poses no synchronization issues. But for GLOBAL mode, the grid expands to 256 blocks per sub-task, and with 4 sub-tasks that's 1024 total blocks. Given typical GPU occupancy limits, only a couple sub-tasks might execute concurrently, though each maintains its own intermediates buffer to avoid conflicts.\n\nThe kernel naturally handles this because each thread's intermediates pointer is already set to the correct per-thread buffer. Within the kernel, threads compute their task offset and stride based on the grid dimensions, so each thread accesses the right slice of its assigned intermediates buffer without collision.\n\nFor multiple sub-tasks on the same stream, they'd reuse the same intermediates buffer and grid layout, but sub-tasks from different threads use separate pre-allocated buffers. Since each sub-task processes different row ranges within the same DAG and intermediates are computed and consumed locally within each row's processing, there's no cross-tile dependency that would break the split.\n\nFor the LIFTED case, after the main kernel runs, there's a scaling operation applied to the output buffer...\n\nApplying vertical repeat to the scaled output...\n\nThese post-processing steps must complete after ALL tiles finish, not per sub-task. If sub-tasks run on different threads, I'd need synchronization before post-processing, which adds complexity. Let me check how often the lifted case actually occurs—at APC 300, most AIRs have height >= 2^l_skip, and with l_skip=0, the lifted height equals the actual height, so the lifted case might not be common.\n\nLooking at the code more carefully, with l_skip=0, lifted_height = max(height, 1) = height, meaning the post-processing branch never triggers. But if l_skip > 0, things change. I need to check what l_skip value is actually passed to the GKR input eval—it's pulled from the prover context, and from the logs I can see the stacked matrix heights vary across different app proof segments.\n\nFor this benchmark, the stacked_matrix height is 2^24 with n_global=24, and since l_skip=0, n_stack=24 and height=2^24, which matches perfectly. So the lifted optimization doesn't apply here—the current single-kernel approach handles it fine.\n\nNow I'm outlining the implementation plan: I need to add a tile_offset parameter to the CUDA kernel and its launcher, update the Rust FFI bindings to include this parameter, define a new work item type that extends the existing one with tile offset and count fields, and then modify the scheduling logic to split GLOBAL-mode AIRs with many tiles into multiple work items when they exceed a threshold.\n\nThe key insight is that each thread gets its own pre-allocated intermediates buffer and can process different tile ranges of the same AIR independently, so multiple work items from one AIR can run on different threads with the same per-AIR data but different tile ranges and buffers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1035,"cache_read_input_tokens":223711,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1035},"output_tokens":13,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"92172169-d9cd-4506-b682-3809f052b557"}
