{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MmwMwomkeXrFVFJg4BwQbV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013zXfoesoRNbumGtaQP6772","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1630-tile-level-gkr-input-multistream/plan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10663,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10663,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BV8r9kKd5y1CDQdWewg3WA","session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"bf0baf0a-4836-4555-b464-19d623a9e922"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013zXfoesoRNbumGtaQP6772","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Tile-Level GKR Input Multi-Stream Distribution\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReduce GKR input evaluation wall time at APC 300 by splitting large-AIR kernel launches into tile-level sub-tasks distributed across multiple CUDA streams. The primary target is segment 0, where input eval takes 241ms due to a few large AIRs (height=2^24) monopolizing single streams.\n6\t\n7\tCurrently, one kernel launch per AIR processes ALL tiles (up to 256 tiles for height=2^24, TASK_SIZE=65536) in an internal loop. This means one thread's stream is occupied for ~30-40ms per large AIR while other streams sit idle. By splitting these into sub-tasks of ≤64 tiles each and distributing across threads, we enable 2x effective GPU concurrency for these bandwidth-bound kernels.\n8\t\n9\tExpected savings: 40-80ms on GKR input eval at APC 300 (primarily segment 0), translating to ~40-80ms improvement on STARK excl trace.\n10\t\n11\t## Current Code Path\n12\t\n13\t### Entry point\n14\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:215` — `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n15\t\n16\t### Work item construction (lines 232-250)\n17\tBuilds one `GkrInputWorkItem` per AIR. Each work item holds references to `air_ctx`, `pk_air`, `l_skip`, `d_challenges`, and `leaves_ptr` (output destination).\n18\t\n19\t### Sorting and pre-allocation (lines 252-321)\n20\tWork items sorted by descending height. Max buffer sizes computed across all items. Per-thread `GkrThreadBuffers` pre-allocated (intermediates, public_values, partition_ptrs, tmp).\n21\t\n22\t### Thread dispatch (lines 328-349)\n23\tItems chunked contiguously: `work_items.chunks(chunk_size)`. Each thread gets a contiguous slice. Worker loop: `for w in chunk { process_gkr_input_air(w, &mut bufs)?; }`\n24\t\n25\t### Per-AIR processing — `process_gkr_input_air()` (lines 112-209)\n26\t1. Collects `partitioned_main` pointers, copies to device (line 156)\n27\t2. Copies `public_values` to device if present (line 142)\n28\t3. Computes `num_rows_per_tile = height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE).max(1)` (line 162)\n29\t4. Launches ONE kernel call: `logup_gkr_input_eval(is_global, ..., height, num_rows_per_tile)` (line 174)\n30\t5. Optional post-process for lifted traces (lines 189-208) — DOES NOT apply at APC 300 (l_skip=0)\n31\t\n32\t### CUDA kernel — `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` (gkr_input.cu:66-208)\n33\tGrid: 256 blocks × 256 threads = TASK_SIZE threads. Internal tile loop:\n34\t```c\n35\tfor (uint32_t j = 0; j < num_rows_per_tile; j++) {\n36\t    uint32_t row = task_offset + j * task_stride;\n37\t    if (row < permutation_height) { /* evaluate DAG, write to d_fracs */ }\n38\t}\n39\t```\n40\tEach tile processes TASK_SIZE rows. For height=2^24: 256 tiles, each taking ~0.15ms = ~38ms total per AIR on one stream.\n41\t\n42\t### CUDA launcher — `_logup_gkr_input_eval()` (gkr_input.cu:216-263)\n43\tLaunches kernel with count=TASK_SIZE for GLOBAL, count=permutation_height for SCATTER.\n44\t\n45\t### Rust FFI — `logup_gkr_input_eval()` (cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs:938-968)\n46\tSafe wrapper around `_logup_gkr_input_eval` extern FFI.\n47\t\n48\t## Changes\n49\t\n50\t### 1. Add tile_offset parameter to CUDA kernel and launcher\n51\t\n52\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.cu`\n53\t\n54\t**Kernel** (line 66): Add `tile_offset` parameter:\n55\t```c\n56\ttemplate <bool GLOBAL>\n57\t__global__ void evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel(\n58\t    FracExt *__restrict__ d_fracs,\n59\t    /* ...existing params... */\n60\t    const uint32_t num_rows_per_tile,\n61\t    const uint32_t tile_offset      // NEW: starting tile index\n62\t)\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\t**Kernel tile loop** (line 94): Change from `j = 0` to `j = tile_offset`:\n66\t```c\n67\tfor (uint32_t j = tile_offset; j < tile_offset + num_rows_per_tile; j++) {\n68\t```\n69\t\n70\t**Launcher** (line 216): Add `tile_offset` parameter, pass through to kernel:\n71\t```c\n72\textern \"C\" int _logup_gkr_input_eval(\n73\t    /* ...existing params... */\n74\t    uint32_t num_rows_per_tile,\n75\t    uint32_t tile_offset             // NEW\n76\t)\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\tPass `tile_offset` to both GLOBAL and SCATTER kernel templates.\n80\t\n81\t**Why**: Enables launching the kernel for a sub-range of tiles. When tile_offset=0 and num_rows_per_tile=total_tiles, behavior is identical to current code.\n82\t\n83\t### 2. Update Rust FFI binding\n84\t\n85\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n86\t\n87\t**Extern declaration** (line 278): Add `tile_offset: u32` parameter to `_logup_gkr_input_eval`.\n88\t\n89\t**Safe wrapper** (line 938): Add `tile_offset: u32` parameter to `logup_gkr_input_eval`, pass through to FFI call.\n90\t\n91\t### 3. Define tile-level work item\n92\t\n93\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n94\t\n95\tAdd a new struct:\n96\t```rust\n97\tstruct GkrInputTileItem<'a, HS: GpuHashScheme> {\n98\t    air: &'a GkrInputWorkItem<'a, HS>,\n99\t    tile_offset: u32,\n100\t    tile_count: u32,\n101\t}\n102\t```\n103\t\n104\tThis references the original per-AIR work item and adds tile range information.\n105\t\n106\t**Why**: Separates per-AIR data (interaction rules, trace pointers) from per-tile scheduling. Multiple tile items can reference the same AIR.\n107\t\n108\t### 4. Split large AIRs into tile items\n109\t\n110\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, in `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n111\t\n112\tAfter building and sorting `work_items` (line 252), add a new phase:\n113\t\n114\t```rust\n115\t// Phase 1b: Split large GLOBAL AIRs into tile-level sub-tasks.\n116\tconst MAX_TILES_PER_ITEM: u32 = 64; // ~4M rows per sub-task\n117\t\n118\tlet mut tile_items: Vec<GkrInputTileItem<HS>> = Vec::new();\n119\tfor w in &work_items {\n120\t    let height = w.air_ctx.height() as u32;\n121\t    let total_tiles = height.div_ceil(TASK_SIZE).max(1);\n122\t    let rules = &w.pk_air.other_data.interaction_rules;\n123\t    let is_global = rules.inner.buffer_size > 10;\n124\t\n125\t    if is_global && total_tiles > MAX_TILES_PER_ITEM && num_threads > 1 {\n126\t        // Split into sub-tasks\n127\t        let mut offset = 0u32;\n128\t        while offset < total_tiles {\n129\t            let count = (total_tiles - offset).min(MAX_TILES_PER_ITEM);\n130\t            tile_items.push(GkrInputTileItem { air: w, tile_offset: offset, tile_count: count });\n131\t            offset += count;\n132\t        }\n133\t    } else {\n134\t        // Keep as single item (small AIRs, SCATTER AIRs, or single-threaded)\n135\t        tile_items.push(GkrInputTileItem { air: w, tile_offset: 0, tile_count: total_tiles });\n136\t    }\n137\t}\n138\t```\n139\t\n140\tSort tile_items by descending tile_count for load balancing (largest sub-tasks first, then round-robin distribution puts them on different threads).\n141\t\n142\t**Why**: Only large GLOBAL AIRs benefit from splitting. Small AIRs and SCATTER AIRs stay as single items to avoid unnecessary overhead. The threshold MAX_TILES_PER_ITEM=64 means sub-tasks process ~4M rows, large enough to amortize kernel launch overhead while small enough to enable 4-way concurrency for the largest AIRs (256 tiles → 4 sub-tasks).\n143\t\n144\t### 5. Adapt per-AIR processing for tile-level dispatch\n145\t\n146\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`\n147\t\n148\tCreate a new function `process_gkr_input_tile()`:\n149\t```rust\n150\tfn process_gkr_input_tile<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n151\t    item: &GkrInputTileItem<HS>,\n152\t    buffers: &mut GkrThreadBuffers,\n153\t) -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n154\t    let w = item.air;\n155\t    // ... same setup as process_gkr_input_air (lines 116-171) ...\n156\t    // BUT: pass tile_offset and tile_count to the kernel:\n157\t    unsafe {\n158\t        logup_gkr_input_eval(\n159\t            is_global,\n160\t            trace_output,\n161\t            d_preprocessed,\n162\t            &buffers.partition_ptrs,\n163\t            d_public_values,\n164\t            w.d_challenges,\n165\t            &buffers.intermediates,\n166\t            &rules.inner.d_rules,\n167\t            &rules.inner.d_used_nodes,\n168\t            &rules.d_pair_idxs,\n169\t            height as u32,\n170\t            item.tile_count,       // was: num_rows_per_tile (total)\n171\t            item.tile_offset,      // NEW\n172\t        )?;\n173\t    }\n174\t    // Skip post-processing (lifted case) — only the LAST tile item\n175\t    // for a given AIR should do post-processing if needed.\n176\t    // At APC 300 with l_skip=0, this branch is never taken.\n177\t    Ok(())\n178\t}\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\tThe per-AIR setup (partition_ptrs H2D, public_values H2D) happens each time a tile item is processed. For a split AIR on 4 threads, this setup is duplicated 4 times. The duplication is acceptable because the H2D copies are tiny (~64 bytes each) and the saved wall time from GPU concurrency far exceeds this overhead.\n182\t\n183\t### 6. Update thread dispatch to use tile items\n184\t\n185\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs`, in `log_gkr_input_evals()`\n186\t\n187\tReplace the current dispatch (lines 328-349) with:\n188\t\n189\t```rust\n190\tif num_threads <= 1 {\n191\t    let mut bufs = thread_buffers.into_iter().next().unwrap();\n192\t    for item in &tile_items {\n193\t        process_gkr_input_tile(item, &mut bufs)?;\n194\t    }\n195\t} else {\n196\t    // Round-robin distribution of tile items across threads\n197\t    let mut per_thread: Vec<Vec<&GkrInputTileItem<HS>>> = (0..num_threads).map(|_| Vec::new()).collect();\n198\t    for (i, item) in tile_items.iter().enumerate() {\n199\t        per_thread[i % num_threads].push(item);\n200\t    }\n201\t\n202\t    std::thread::scope(|s| {\n203\t        let handles: Vec<_> = thread_buffers\n204\t            .into_iter()\n205\t            .zip(per_thread)\n206\t            .map(|(mut bufs, items)| {\n207\t                s.spawn(move || -> Result<(), InteractionGpuError> {\n208\t                    for item in items {\n209\t                        process_gkr_input_tile(item, &mut bufs)?;\n210\t                    }\n211\t                    current_stream_sync().map_err(InteractionGpuError::from)?;\n212\t                    Ok(())\n213\t                })\n214\t            })\n215\t            .collect();\n216\t        for handle in handles {\n217\t            handle.join().unwrap()?;\n218\t        }\n219\t        Ok::<_, InteractionGpuError>(())\n220\t    })?;\n221\t}\n222\t```\n223\t\n224\t**Why**: Round-robin on the sorted tile_items (largest first) naturally distributes large sub-tasks across different threads, preventing any single thread from accumulating too much work.\n225\t\n226\t## Invariants\n227\t\n228\t1. **Correctness**: Each row is processed exactly once. With tile_offset and tile_count partitioning the [0, total_tiles) range, every row in [0, permutation_height) is covered exactly once across all sub-tasks.\n229\t\n230\t2. **Output writes**: `d_fracs[out_idx]` where `out_idx = (interaction_idx * permutation_height + row) * 2`. Each sub-task writes to non-overlapping row ranges. No data race.\n231\t\n232\t3. **Intermediates isolation**: Each thread uses its own pre-allocated intermediates buffer. Sub-tasks of the same AIR on different threads use different buffers. The intermediates are computed and consumed within each row (no cross-row dependency).\n233\t\n234\t4. **leaves buffer initialization**: `fill_zero()` + `current_stream_sync()` happens before any thread writes. Sub-tasks write to disjoint regions.\n235\t\n236\t5. **No APC 0 regression**: APC 0 has <100 AIRs → single-threaded path. Even in multi-threaded mode, small AIRs are not split (total_tiles <= MAX_TILES_PER_ITEM). Behavior is identical to current code.\n237\t\n238\t6. **Lifted trace correctness**: At APC 300 with l_skip=0, the lifted case (height != lifted_height) is never triggered. For safety, add a guard: if l_skip > 0 and height != lifted_height, don't split that AIR (keep as a single item). This ensures the post-processing step runs on a single thread with the complete output.\n239\t\n240\t## Measurement Plan\n241\t\n242\t1. Build and run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 300}\n243\t2. Compare STARK excl trace and LogUp GKR at APC 300 vs before (1302ms, 522ms)\n244\t3. Compare per-segment GKR input eval timing from metrics (`prover.rap_constraints.logup_gkr.input_evals_time_ms`)\n245\t4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression\n246\t5. Profile with nsys to confirm increased concurrent kernel execution for large AIRs\n247\t\n248\t**Success criteria**: LogUp GKR at APC 300 improves by ≥30ms (from 522ms to ≤492ms). STARK excl trace ≥30ms improvement. No APC 0 regression.\n249\t\n250\t## Rollback Criteria\n251\t\n252\t- LogUp GKR improvement at APC 300 is <30ms\n253\t- Any regression at APC 0 exceeding 15ms\n254\t- Correctness failure (proof verification fails)\n255\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BV8r9kKd5y1CDQdWewg3WA","session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"aaf1ab21-0b4e-4a8d-9297-c5c10d7757f0","timestamp":"2026-04-14T14:46:07.982Z"}
