          .into_iter()\n                .flatten()\n                .collect_vec();\n            let mut output_mats = batch_fold(input_mats)?.into_iter();\n            lengths\n                .into_iter()\n                .map(|len| output_mats.by_ref().take(len).collect())\n                .collect()\n        };\n        if self.save_memory {\n            self.memory_limit_bytes = self.gkr_mem_contribution.saturating_sub(\n                self.mat_evals_per_trace\n                    .iter()\n                    .flatten()\n                    .map(|m| m.buffer().len() * size_of::<EF>())\n                    .sum(),\n            );\n        }\n\n        // Fold sels_per_trace: Vec<DeviceMatrix<EF>>\n        self.sels_per_trace = batch_fold(std::mem::take(&mut self.sels_per_trace))?;\n\n        for tree in self.eq_xis.values_mut() {\n            // trim the back (which corresponds to r_{j-1}) because we don't need it anymore\n            if tree.layers.len() > 1 {\n                tree.layers.pop();\n            }\n        }\n        let xi = self.xi[self.l_skip + round - 1];\n        let eq_r = eval_eq_mle(&[xi], &[r_round]);\n        self.eq_ns.push(self.eq_ns[round - 1] * eq_r);\n        self.eq_sharp_ns.push(self.eq_sharp_ns[round - 1] * eq_r);\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    #[instrument(\n        name = \"LogupZerocheck::into_column_openings\",\n        level = \"debug\",\n        skip_all\n    )]\n    fn into_column_openings(mut self) -> Result<Vec<Vec<Vec<EF>>>, LogupZerocheckError> {","numLines":100,"startLine":1999,"totalLines":2197}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DK7iT6WzKzmCgoQ4PNoNXr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see — the fold creates new DeviceMatrix for each trace per round (line 2014). The batch_fold closure:\n1. Creates num_matrices new DeviceMatrix outputs (one per foldable matrix)\n2. Collects input/output pointers\n3. Uploads pointers to GPU\n4. Launches batch_fold_mle kernel\n5. Returns the new output matrices (which become the next round's input)\n\nThe old input matrices are dropped when `std::mem::take(&mut self.mat_evals_per_trace)` is consumed.\n\n\nNow I'm looking at the memory pressure during the constraints MLE fold — with 623 traces, I'm allocating somewhere between 1400 and 2400 DeviceMatrix objects per round, with an equal number of deallocations happening as the fold progresses through each iteration.\n\nAcross the full 24-round protocol, that's roughly 34K to 58K allocation and deallocation operations. Based on the bulk-alloc benchmarking I did earlier, I'm seeing about 37K allocations per run, which translates to around 15 milliseconds of overhead — and I managed to recover about 13 milliseconds from pooling in that previous task.\n\nI'm hitting a wall here. The GKR input evaluation is fundamentally bandwidth-bound and can't be parallelized further, the fractional sumcheck is inherently sequential by protocol design, and the MLE fold allocations only save about 13 milliseconds. The segment overlap optimization is outside what I can do in stark-backend, and meaningful kernel optimizations would require deep CUDA expertise I don't have. So I need to refocus on the core goal: making the STARK exclusion trace scale with the workload reduction. Right now I'm seeing a 1.89x speedup at APC 300 (2455ms down to 1302ms), but I need to push that further.\n\nThe real problem is that certain components aren't scaling at all. Round 0 stays flat at 177 milliseconds, the MLE rounds actually get worse at 425 milliseconds total, and stacked reduction is stuck at 75 milliseconds — together that's 425 milliseconds or about a third of the total time at APC 300. These are the fixed overheads killing my scaling potential. In Round 0 specifically, the per-AIR overhead dominates — with 623 AIRs at APC 300 versus 99 at APC 0, I'd expect the overhead to grow by 6.3x, but multi-streaming across 8 threads masks some of that. The real issue is that more AIRs means more kernel launches and host-to-device copies, which don't scale down with the actual computation.\n\nThe MLE rounds are similar — more AIRs means more traces per batch, more descriptor building, more fold operations. The fundamental trade-off is that at APC 300, I'm getting fewer cells but way more AIRs. The cell count drops 2.35x while AIR count jumps 6.3x, so the per-AIR overhead never gets negligible enough. What if instead of processing each AIR separately, I could merge multiple small AIRs into larger batch operations — essentially creating \"mega-AIRs\" that process together and amortize that overhead across more work?\n\nI've already tried batching where it was feasible — the array kernel approach for Round 0 failed due to CUDA memory access issues from the Rust optimizer, and the MLE rounds are already batched in a single kernel. The GKR input evaluation is multi-streamed. So the real bottleneck is on the CPU side — the host loops, small host-to-device copies, and descriptor building that happens per-AIR. The most impactful fix would be pre-computing all the static per-AIR metadata at key generation time instead of in the hot proof path, which includes the interaction rules, DAG structure, and buffer sizes that don't change between proofs.\n\nLooking more carefully at what's actually happening in the sumcheck polynomial batch evaluation per round, I'm seeing that the CPU is doing metadata collection for each trace, gathering column pointers, setting up batch interpolation, and processing late and early evaluations — this loop is where the per-segment overhead is accumulating.\n\nThe loop iterates over all 623 traces per round, spending roughly half a microsecond per trace on metadata building and pointer collection, which adds up to about 0.3-0.6ms per round. Across 24 rounds that's 7-14ms total, but if I pre-computed the static parts (which traces are Case A versus B, their metadata structure) and only updated the dynamic parts after each fold, I could cut this roughly in half to 4-7ms.\n\nThat's still not a huge win though. I'm thinking the ping-pong fold optimization for the constraints MLE might be worth revisiting — even though it was reverted before, a cleaner implementation could save 15-20ms and the core idea is sound. But I need to think through what the reviewer's original concern was.\n\nThey said the tile-splitting plan was mechanically correct and implementable, just that my performance hypothesis wasn't validated. They wanted me to measure actual per-kernel bandwidth utilization first. So instead of justifying tile-level splitting as a way to improve GPU concurrency for bandwidth-bound kernels, what if I frame it differently — as a load balancing strategy for thread scheduling? The win comes from reducing the critical path thread's workload, not from GPU-level parallelism. Right now thread 0 gets all the large AIRs and its wall time is roughly the sum of their kernel times plus overhead, around 200ms. With tile-level splitting, thread 0 only handles about a quarter of each large AIR's tiles, so its wall time drops to roughly a quarter of that sum plus overhead, around 55ms. The GPU itself still...\n\nActually, I need to think through this more carefully. When thread 0 submits 200ms of GPU work on its stream and then syncs, it waits 200ms. But if I split that work across 4 threads, each thread submits only 50ms on their own stream. The GPU processes all the work serially since it's bandwidth-bound, taking 200ms total. But here's the key: thread 0's stream only has 50ms of work queued, so when thread 0 syncs, it only waits 50ms before its stream is done — not the full 200ms. Same for threads 1, 2, and 3. They each sync after 50ms. The GPU still takes 200ms total to process everything, but the individual threads are unblocked much sooner because they're only waiting on their own stream's portion of the work.\n\nThe join at the end still waits for all threads, so the wall time is still 200ms. But the key insight is that the threads themselves aren't blocked for the full duration — they're only blocked for their individual stream's execution time, which is much shorter. This could matter if there's other work they could do between the sync and the join, or if the synchronization overhead is significant. The GPU scheduler assigns each stream's next ready kernel to available SMs, but with bandwidth-bound kernels only one runs at a time, so execution order depends on submission order. Currently, thread 0 submits large kernels that dominate GPU time (~200ms total), while thread 7's tiny kernels queue behind and might finish around 10ms if they slip between thread 0's work. With tile-splitting, thread 0 would submit smaller tiles instead, allowing better interleaving.\n\nLooking at the tile-split scenario: thread 0 submits 10ms tiles interleaved with small AIR kernels, and thread 1 does the same with its tiles. The GPU scheduler interleaves these across all threads, so thread 0's last kernel completes around 180ms instead of 200ms, and thread 7 finishes similarly. The join happens at roughly 190ms—maybe a 5% improvement from better interleaving, but that's not significant enough to justify the complexity.\n\nI'm now convinced tile-level splitting won't provide meaningful gains.\n\nLet me pivot to a completely different approach. After thorough analysis, the most viable optimization that could exceed the rollback threshold is pre-computing the fold output buffer layout and replacing per-round DeviceMatrix allocations with pre-sized ping-pong buffers. This directly tackles the non-scaling MLE Rounds overhead—173ms at APC 300 versus 113ms at APC 0—where the per-round allocation overhead of ~15ms is a substantial chunk of that 173ms.\n\nThe implementation is straightforward: compute total cells before the MLE loop, allocate two contiguous DeviceBuffers, then each round uses batch_fold with pointer offsets to move data between buffers and swap them, eliminating per-round allocation and deallocation overhead.\n\nThis eliminates tens of thousands of cudaMallocAsync and cudaFreeAsync calls while avoiding the ArenaMatrix complexity from earlier attempts, using simple pointer arithmetic and offset tuples instead of creating new DeviceMatrix objects each round. The expected savings of 15-25ms should exceed the previous 13ms estimate, with high probability since it's mechanically straightforward, though managing pointer arithmetic requires care.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5344,"cache_read_input_tokens":243844,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5344},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"d0dc8561-87b9-4b34-9d42-ffde4cfe7677"}
