s that dominate segment 0's 241ms wall time.","+Replace per-round DeviceMatrix allocations in the constraints-phase MLE fold (`fold_mle_evals`) with pre-allocated contiguous ping-pong buffers. Currently, each MLE round allocates ~1400 new DeviceMatrix objects (one per foldable trace matrix across mat_evals and sels) and frees the previous round's ~1400 matrices, totaling ~37K cudaMallocAsync + ~37K cudaFreeAsync calls across 24 MLE rounds at APC 300. By pre-allocating two contiguous buffers (one for even rounds, one for odd) and computing per-trace offsets by halving the previous round's layout, we eliminate all per-round allocation/deallocation overhead (~15-20ms) while preserving the existing batched fold kernel."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-14-1630-tile-level-gkr-input-multistream\n\nSplit large-AIR GKR input evaluation kernel launches into tile-level sub-tasks distributed across multiple CUDA streams. Currently, each AIR is processed as a single unit on one thread's stream, meaning a large AIR with 256 tiles (height=2^24, TASK_SIZE=65536) monopolizes one stream for ~30-40ms while other streams sit idle. By splitting large AIRs into sub-tasks of ≤64 tiles and distributing them round-robin, multiple sub-tasks from the same AIR execute concurrently on different streams, improving GPU utilization from ~1x to ~2x effective parallelism for the bandwidth-bound kernels that dominate segment 0's 241ms wall time.\n"}}
