{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WaVSmfamjNFG9dH6uYuYYL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01494RbrvtQdYFhTV391TJoj","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1630-pingpong-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Ping-Pong MLE Fold Buffers\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate ~37K per-benchmark DeviceMatrix allocations and ~37K frees in the constraints-phase MLE fold (`fold_mle_evals`) by replacing them with two pre-allocated contiguous device buffers used in a ping-pong pattern. The MLE Rounds at APC 300 take 173ms, of which ~15ms is CUDA memory pool API overhead from these allocations. The optimization removes this overhead without introducing new types or abstractions.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### MLE Rounds loop\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:631-648`\n```rust\nfor round in 1..=n_max {\n    let sp_round_evals = prover.sumcheck_polys_batch_eval(round, r[round - 1])?;\n    // ... transcript observe, sample r_round ...\n    prover.fold_mle_evals(round, r_round)?;\n}\n```\n\n### fold_mle_evals\n`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1999-2091`\n\nContains a `batch_fold` closure (lines 2002-2048) that:\n1. **Counts foldable matrices** (height > 1) via `partition_point` (line 2003)\n2. **Allocates output matrices**: For each foldable matrix, creates a NEW `DeviceMatrix<EF>::with_capacity(output_height, width)` (line 2014). Each call → `cudaMallocAsync`.\n3. **Collects input/output pointers** into Vecs (lines 2019-2027)\n4. **Uploads pointer arrays** to device: 4 H2D copies (input_ptrs, output_ptrs, log_output_heights, widths) (lines 2029-2032)\n5. **Launches batch_fold_mle kernel** (lines 2034-2044): single kernel processes all matrices\n6. **Extends with non-foldable** matrices (height=1) at the end (line 2046)\n7. **Returns new output matrices**, dropping old inputs → `cudaFreeAsync` for each.\n\nCalled twice per round:\n- `mat_evals_per_trace` (lines 2050-2066): ~800 matrices at APC 300 (flattened from 623 traces × 1-3 matrices each)\n- `sels_per_trace` (line 2078): ~623 matrices\n\nTotal per round: ~1400 DeviceMatrix allocs + ~1400 frees = ~2800 CUDA API calls.\nTotal per benchmark (24 rounds × 2 segments): ~2800 × 48 ≈ 134K API calls, of which ~67K are allocs and ~67K are frees.\n\nAt ~0.3-0.5μs per CUDA pool API call: **~40-67ms total overhead**.\n\nThe previous bulk-alloc task recovered 13ms of this using ArenaMatrix/FoldArena types. It was reverted because 13ms was below the 20ms threshold, and the added type complexity (ArenaMatrix, MatrixRef, FoldArena) was not justified.\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. Pre-compute the fold layout before the MLE loop\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nAfter the fold_ple_evals call (line 617) and before the MLE loop (line 631), compute:\n\n```rust\n// Pre-compute per-matrix sizes for ping-pong allocation.\n// mat_evals are stored flattened; sels are already flat.\nlet mat_sizes: Vec<(usize, usize)> = self.mat_evals_per_trace\n    .iter()\n    .flatten()\n    .map(|m| (m.height(), m.width()))\n    .collect();\nlet sel_sizes: Vec<(usize, usize)> = self.sels_per_trace\n    .iter()\n    .map(|m| (m.height(), m.width()))\n    .collect();\n\n// Total cells for the initial round (max size needed for either buffer).\nlet mat_total_cells: usize = mat_sizes.iter().map(|(h, w)| h * w).sum();\nlet sel_total_cells: usize = sel_sizes.iter().map(|(h, w)| h * w).sum();\n\n// Allocate two ping-pong buffers for mat_evals and two for sels.\nlet mut mat_buf_a = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(mat_total_cells);\nlet mut mat_buf_b = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(mat_total_cells / 2);\nlet mut sel_buf_a = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(sel_total_cells);\nlet mut sel_buf_b = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(sel_total_cells / 2);\n```\n\n**Why**: Buffer A holds the current round's data (full size). Buffer B holds the next round's folded data (half size). After swapping, B becomes the input (half size) and A becomes the output (quarter size). A is always large enough because it was originally full size.\n\nNote: `mat_total_cells / 2` is sufficient for B because each fold halves the height. After the first fold, total cells become `mat_total_cells / 2`. After the second, `mat_total_cells / 4`. Buffer A (original full size) always suffices.\n\n### 2. Copy initial mat_evals and sels into buffer A\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nAfter allocating ping-pong buffers:\n\n```rust\n// Pack current mat_evals into buffer A, recording per-matrix offsets.\nlet mut mat_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::with_capacity(mat_sizes.len());\nlet mut offset = 0usize;\nfor m in self.mat_evals_per_trace.iter().flatten() {\n    // D2D copy from m's buffer into mat_buf_a at offset\n    cuda_memcpy_d2d(\n        mat_buf_a.as_mut_ptr().add(offset),\n        m.buffer().as_ptr(),\n        m.buffer().len(),\n    );\n    mat_offsets.push(offset);\n    offset += m.buffer().len();\n}\n// Similar for sels into sel_buf_a\nlet mut sel_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::with_capacity(sel_sizes.len());\noffset = 0;\nfor m in &self.sels_per_trace {\n    cuda_memcpy_d2d(sel_buf_a.as_mut_ptr().add(offset), m.buffer().as_ptr(), m.buffer().len());\n    sel_offsets.push(offset);\n    offset += m.buffer().len();\n}\n```\n\nThen drop the original DeviceMatrix vectors to free GPU memory:\n```rust\nself.mat_evals_per_trace = vec![]; // free original per-matrix buffers\nself.sels_per_trace = vec![];\n```\n\n**Why**: Packing into contiguous buffers lets us use pointer arithmetic for per-matrix views without per-round allocations. The D2D copies are a one-time cost (~2-3ms) amortized across 24 rounds.\n\n### 3. Replace fold_mle_evals with ping-pong fold\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nReplace `fold_mle_evals` with a new method that uses the ping-pong buffers:\n\n```rust\nfn fold_mle_evals_pingpong(\n    &mut self,\n    round: usize,\n    r_round: EF,\n    mat_sizes: &mut Vec<(usize, usize)>,\n    sel_sizes: &mut Vec<(usize, usize)>,\n    mat_offsets: &mut Vec<usize>,\n    sel_offsets: &mut Vec<usize>,\n    buf_a: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    buf_b: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    sel_buf_a: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n    sel_buf_b: &mut DeviceBuffer<EF>,\n) -> Result<(), LogupZerocheckError> {\n    // Batch fold mat_evals: compute output pointers from buf_b offsets\n    let num_foldable_mats = mat_sizes.partition_point(|(h, _)| *h > 1);\n    let mut output_offset = 0usize;\n    let input_ptrs: Vec<_> = (0..num_foldable_mats)\n        .map(|i| unsafe { buf_a.as_ptr().add(mat_offsets[i]) })\n        .collect();\n    let output_ptrs: Vec<_> = (0..num_foldable_mats)\n        .map(|i| {\n            let ptr = unsafe { buf_b.as_mut_ptr().add(output_offset) };\n            let (h, w) = mat_sizes[i];\n            output_offset += (h / 2) * w;\n            ptr\n        })\n        .collect();\n    let log_output_heights: Vec<u8> = (0..num_foldable_mats)\n        .map(|i| (mat_sizes[i].0 / 2).ilog2() as u8)\n        .collect();\n    let widths: Vec<u32> = (0..num_foldable_mats)\n        .map(|i| mat_sizes[i].1 as u32)\n        .collect();\n    let max_output_cells = (0..num_foldable_mats)\n        .map(|i| (mat_sizes[i].0 / 2) * mat_sizes[i].1)\n        .max()\n        .unwrap_or(0);\n\n    // Upload pointer arrays and launch batch fold kernel\n    let d_input_ptrs = input_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_output_ptrs = output_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    let d_log_heights = log_output_heights.to_device()?;\n    let d_widths = widths.to_device()?;\n    unsafe {\n        batch_fold_mle(\n            &d_input_ptrs, &d_output_ptrs, &d_widths,\n            num_foldable_mats as u32, &d_log_heights,\n            max_output_cells as u32, r_round,\n        )?;\n    }\n\n    // Update mat_sizes and mat_offsets for next round\n    output_offset = 0;\n    for i in 0..num_foldable_mats {\n        mat_sizes[i].0 /= 2;\n        mat_offsets[i] = output_offset;\n        output_offset += mat_sizes[i].0 * mat_sizes[i].1;\n    }\n    // Non-foldable matrices (height=1) stay in buf_a; copy them to buf_b\n    for i in num_foldable_mats..mat_sizes.len() {\n        let cells = mat_sizes[i].0 * mat_sizes[i].1;\n        unsafe {\n            cuda_memcpy_d2d(buf_b.as_mut_ptr().add(output_offset),\n                           buf_a.as_ptr().add(mat_offsets[i]), cells);\n        }\n        mat_offsets[i] = output_offset;\n        output_offset += cells;\n    }\n\n    // Swap buffers\n    std::mem::swap(buf_a, buf_b);\n\n    // Same for sels (analogous logic)\n    // ... [similar code for sel_buf_a, sel_buf_b, sel_sizes, sel_offsets] ...\n\n    // Update eq_xis (trim layers) — unchanged from current code\n    for tree in self.eq_xis.values_mut() {\n        if tree.layers.len() > 1 { tree.layers.pop(); }\n    }\n    let xi = self.xi[self.l_skip + round - 1];\n    let eq_r = eval_eq_mle(&[xi], &[r_round]);\n    self.eq_ns.push(self.eq_ns[round - 1] * eq_r);\n    self.eq_sharp_ns.push(self.eq_sharp_ns[round - 1] * eq_r);\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\n**Why**: No per-round DeviceMatrix allocation or freeing. The batch_fold_mle kernel writes directly into the ping-pong buffer. Only 4 small H2D uploads per fold call (pointer arrays, heights, widths) — same as current code.\n\n### 4. Update sumcheck_polys_batch_eval to read from ping-pong buffers\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, function `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`\n\nCurrently reads from `self.mat_evals_per_trace` and `self.sels_per_trace` (DeviceMatrix objects). With ping-pong buffers, these are replaced by raw pointers + offsets.\n\nAdd a helper method that returns (ptr, height, width) for a given matrix index:\n```rust\nfn mat_eval_view(&self, trace_idx: usize, mat_idx: usize,\n                  mat_sizes: &[(usize, usize)], mat_offsets: &[usize],\n                  buf: &DeviceBuffer<EF>) -> (*const EF, usize, usize) {\n    let flat_idx = /* compute from per_trace_lengths and mat_idx */;\n    let (h, w) = mat_sizes[flat_idx];\n    let ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(mat_offsets[flat_idx]) };\n    (ptr, h, w)\n}\n```\n\nUpdate the references in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval to use these views instead of `self.mat_evals_per_trace[trace_idx][mat_idx]`.\n\n### 5. Update into_column_openings to read from ping-pong buffer\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, function `into_column_openings` (line 2098)\n\nCurrently calls `m.to_host()` on each DeviceMatrix. Replace with manual D2H from ping-pong buffer:\n```rust\nlet host_data = cuda_memcpy_d2h(buf.as_ptr().add(offset), h * w);\nColMajorMatrix::new(host_data, w)\n```\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Fold correctness**: The batch_fold_mle kernel receives the same (input_ptr, output_ptr, height, width) data as before. Only the source of pointers changes (pre-allocated buffer + offset vs DeviceMatrix::buffer()).\n\n2. **Buffer sizing**: Buffer A starts at full size. After fold, data in B is half the size. After swap, A (now the old B, half size) is the input. B (now the old A, full size) has more than enough capacity for the output (quarter size). This invariant holds for all rounds.\n\n3. **Non-foldable matrices**: Matrices with height=1 don't get folded. They are D2D-copied from A to B to maintain a consistent single-buffer layout after each round.\n\n4. **Memory accounting**: The `save_memory` path (lines 2067-2075) tracked `mat_evals_per_trace` buffer sizes for memory limit adjustment. With ping-pong buffers, compute the total from `mat_sizes` instead.\n\n5. **No APC 0 regression**: At APC 0, there are 99 AIRs with fewer matrices. The ping-pong allocation is still beneficial (fewer allocs to save, but also less overhead from the ping-pong setup). The setup cost (~2ms for D2D packing) is amortized across rounds.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n1. Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 300}\n2. Compare MLE Rounds time at APC 300: expect 173ms → ~155-160ms\n3. Compare STARK excl trace at APC 300: expect 1302ms → ~1282-1287ms (15-20ms improvement)\n4. Verify APC 0 shows no regression (MLE Rounds should stay at ~113ms or improve slightly)\n5. Verify correctness: proof verification succeeds at all APC configs\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\n- MLE Rounds improvement at APC 300 is <10ms\n- STARK excl trace improvement at APC 300 is <10ms\n- Any regression at APC 0 exceeding 10ms\n- Correctness failure (proof verification fails)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":161,"cache_read_input_tokens":252772,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":161},"output_tokens":67,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8f518d6-4de0-4c05-9b5a-ade72ff81f39","uuid":"e0d1bbb9-9424-42e6-afcd-591ea44f6a14"}
