. m.width())`) read per-matrix widths from `DeviceMatrix`. With ping-pong buffers, widths must come from `mat_fold_sizes`. The plan's `mat_view` returns `(ptr, h, w)` but the Phase 3 access pattern requires iterating over a per-trace slice of the sizes array, not just looking up a single flat index. The plan should specify that `mat_per_trace_counts` prefix sums are needed to reconstruct the per-trace slicing for all access sites, not just the ones listed.\n\n### 8. Buffer sizing: buffer B can be smaller than buffer A\n\nThe plan allocates `mat_buf_b` with the same capacity as `mat_buf_a` (Section 2, line 97: `DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(mat_total)`). After round 1, the output in B has at most `mat_total / 2` cells (since foldable heights halve and non-foldable heights stay at 1). Buffer B never needs more than `mat_total` capacity, so this is correct but wastes memory. Since the ping-pong swap means both buffers are used as source and destination alternately, and the first fold writes at most `mat_total` cells (some heights halve, some stay), the full-size allocation for both is safe. This is a cleanup observation, not a blocker.\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### A. Skip initial D2D packing entirely\n\nInstead of copying existing `DeviceMatrix` data into buffer A via D2D copies (Section 2), the fold could operate on the original `DeviceMatrix` objects for round 1 only (read from the originals, write into buffer B), then switch to ping-pong for subsequent rounds. This saves the ~2-3ms packing cost and avoids the complexity of the initialization step. The fold kernel already accepts pointer arrays, so passing original `DeviceMatrix` pointers as input and ping-pong buffer slices as output works without any kernel changes.\n\n### B. Use `ManuallyDrop` for the D2H reads in `into_column_openings`\n\nRather than inventing a new `into_column_openings_pingpong` method, wrap a `ManuallyDrop<DeviceBuffer<EF>>` around `from_raw_parts` so the existing `to_host()` trait works without freeing. This is a one-line fix:\n```rust\nlet view = ManuallyDrop::new(unsafe { DeviceBuffer::<EF>::from_raw_parts(ptr as *mut EF, h * w) });\nlet data = view.to_host()?;\n```\n\n### C. Combine `mat_fold_sizes` and `mat_fold_offsets` into a single struct\n\nA `MatSlice { offset: usize, height: usize, width: usize }` struct would be clearer than parallel arrays and eliminates the risk of index mismatch between sizes and offsets. This does not affect performance.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere is one design-level blocker: the `from_raw_parts` double-free in `into_column_openings_pingpong` (Finding 1). This must be fixed before prototyping -- either use `ManuallyDrop` or call `cuda_memcpy` directly.\n\nThere is one implementation gap: the non-existent `cuda_memcpy_d2d` function (Finding 2). The fix is straightforward (use the existing generic `cuda_memcpy::<true, true>`).\n\nThe `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` update (Findings 6, 7) is underspecified but not a blocker -- all the required information (flat indices, per-trace counts, sizes) is available; the plan just needs to spell out the Case B column-stride arithmetic and the Phase 3 width-access pattern more explicitly.\n\nAfter fixing these issues, the plan is prototype-ready. The strongest part is the simplicity relative to the previous ArenaMatrix/MatrixRef/FoldArena approach: raw buffers with offset/size arrays are easier to reason about and add zero new types to the codebase. The performance estimate of ~15-20ms is well-grounded in the previous task's confirmed 13ms recovery (which used a heavier approach that had its own overhead), and the simpler buffer-swap avoids the metadata bookkeeping that ate into the previous task's savings. The expected net improvement of 10-15ms on MLE Rounds at APC 300 is realistic, though it remains close to the 10ms rollback threshold."}],"totalDurationMs":226875,"totalTokens":60054,"totalToolUseCount":46,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":420,"ca